const Q = [
{"c":"lead","q":"How do you design a scalable test automation framework from scratch?","a":"\n<p>Start with a <strong>layered architecture</strong> so each layer changes for one reason:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Test layer</strong> — business intent only, no locators, no waits.</li>\n<li><strong>Page Objects / API clients</strong> — UI interactions and request builders.</li>\n<li><strong>Core</strong> — driver factory, waits, ThreadLocal management, custom exceptions.</li>\n<li><strong>Utilities</strong> — data readers (JSON/Excel/DB), config manager, DB and file helpers.</li>\n<li><strong>Reporting</strong> — Extent/Allure via listeners, never hard-wired into tests.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Then bolt on: externalised config per environment, parallel execution, CI integration, and versioned test data. Design for the person maintaining it in year two.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Talk about adoption, not just architecture — coding standards, PR reviews, onboarding docs, and how you got manual testers contributing.</div>"},
{"c":"lead","q":"How do you decide what to automate and what stays manual?","a":"\n<p><strong>Automate:</strong> regression, smoke, data-driven flows, high-risk business paths, and anything run more than a few times with stable requirements.</p>\n<p><strong>Keep manual:</strong> exploratory, usability, accessibility judgement calls, one-off validations, and volatile features still changing weekly.</p>\n<p>The lens is ROI — (execution frequency × manual effort) versus (build cost + maintenance cost). A test run twice a year rarely pays back.</p>"},
{"c":"lead","q":"How do you measure and report automation effectiveness?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Coverage</strong> — % of regression suite automated, and % of critical business flows covered.</li>\n<li><strong>Pass-rate trend</strong> — stability over time, not a single run.</li>\n<li><strong>Flaky rate</strong> — tests that needed a retry to pass.</li>\n<li><strong>Defect detection</strong> — bugs caught by automation before UAT/prod.</li>\n<li><strong>Cycle time saved</strong> — regression hours reduced per release.</li>\n<li><strong>Maintenance cost</strong> — hours spent fixing scripts per sprint.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Report to stakeholders in risk and release-confidence terms, not script counts. Prune low-value tests every quarter.</p>"},
{"c":"lead","q":"Regression takes 6 hours. How do you bring it down?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Parallelise</strong> — threads locally, sharding across CI agents/Grid.</li>\n<li><strong>Tier the suite</strong> — smoke (&lt;15 min on every commit), sanity, full regression nightly.</li>\n<li><strong>Push validations down the pyramid</strong> — move UI checks to API tests where the assertion doesn't need the browser; API is roughly 10× faster.</li>\n<li><strong>Set up data via API</strong> instead of clicking through the UI.</li>\n<li><strong>Remove duplicates</strong> — many suites validate the same login 40 times.</li>\n<li><strong>Test impact analysis</strong> — run only what the code change touches.</li>\n<li><strong>Headless</strong> for non-visual checks.</li>\n</ul>"},
{"c":"lead","q":"What's your strategy for test data management?","a":"\n<p>Keep data <strong>external and environment-scoped</strong> — JSON/Excel/DB/faker, never hardcoded in tests. Prefer creating data through API setup calls before the test and cleaning up after, so tests are self-sufficient.</p>\n<p>On shared environments, generate unique identifiers (UUID/timestamp) to avoid collisions between parallel threads and between teams. For data you can't create — legacy accounts, funded policies — maintain a reserved pool with a lease/release mechanism.</p>"},
{"c":"lead","q":"How do you handle flaky tests as a lead?","a":"\n<p>Treat flakiness as a defect with an owner, not background noise:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Measure it</strong> — tag every retried test in reports, track a weekly flaky list.</li>\n<li><strong>Classify the cause</strong> — timing/sync, test data collision, environment instability, genuine app race condition.</li>\n<li><strong>Fix the class, not the instance</strong> — a bad wait pattern usually repeats across 30 tests.</li>\n<li><strong>Quarantine</strong> persistent offenders out of the gating suite so the pipeline stays trustworthy — with a deadline to fix, not forever.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Say this</b>A red build people ignore is worse than no build. Trust in the suite is the real asset.</div>"},
{"c":"lead","q":"How do you manage reporting across a large suite?","a":"\n<p>Reports are generated by listeners, never by test code. Extent or Allure with: test steps, request/response logs for API tests, screenshot on failure, environment banner (env, browser, build number), and history/trend if the CI plugin supports it.</p>\n<p>For thread safety, hold the report object in a ThreadLocal. Publish HTML as a pipeline artifact and also emit JUnit XML so the CI tool's own test dashboard works.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>The audience matters — devs want the stack trace, the release manager wants one line: is it safe to ship?</div>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Difference between List, Set and Map — where do you use each in automation?","a":"\n<table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Behaviour</th><th>Automation use</th></tr>\n<tr><td>List</td><td>Ordered, duplicates allowed</td><td>Results of <code>findElements()</code>, verifying dropdown order</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Set</td><td>No duplicates, unordered (LinkedHashSet keeps order)</td><td>Duplicate detection: <code>assertEquals(list.size(), new HashSet&lt;&gt;(list).size())</code>; window handles</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Map</td><td>Key–value</td><td>Test data rows, headers, config properties, JSON payloads</td></tr>\n</table>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Why ThreadLocal instead of a static WebDriver for parallel runs?","a":"\n<p><code>static</code> means one copy shared by every instance — fine for utilities and constants, fatal for a driver. In parallel execution all threads would drive the same browser session and stamp on each other.</p>\n<pre><code>public class DriverFactory {\n    private static ThreadLocal&lt;WebDriver&gt; tlDriver = new ThreadLocal&lt;&gt;();\n\n    public static void set(WebDriver driver) { tlDriver.set(driver); }\n    public static WebDriver get()            { return tlDriver.get(); }\n\n    public static void unload() {\n        if (tlDriver.get() != null) tlDriver.get().quit();\n        tlDriver.remove();          // prevents memory leak in thread pools\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p><code>static</code> gives global access; ThreadLocal gives each thread its own isolated copy. Always call <code>remove()</code> in teardown.</p>"},
{"c":"java","q":"How do you use exception handling in a framework?","a":"\n<p><code>try-catch-finally</code> plus custom exceptions for readable failures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Catch <code>StaleElementReferenceException</code> in wrapper methods and re-find the element once before failing.</li>\n<li><code>finally</code> / try-with-resources to close drivers, DB connections and file streams.</li>\n<li>Custom <code>FrameworkException</code> so a config typo doesn't surface as a raw NullPointerException.</li>\n<li><strong>Checked</strong> (IOException reading Excel) must be handled or declared; <strong>unchecked</strong> (NPE, NoSuchElement) happen at runtime.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Watch out</b>Never swallow an exception with an empty catch block to make a test \"pass\" — that's how false green suites are born.</div>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Which Java 8+ features do you use in automation?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lambdas</strong> — custom fluent waits: <code>wait.until(d -&gt; d.findElement(by).isDisplayed());</code></li>\n<li><strong>Streams</strong> — <code>elements.stream().map(WebElement::getText).collect(Collectors.toList());</code></li>\n<li><strong>Optional</strong> — safe access to nullable API response fields.</li>\n<li><strong>Method references</strong> — <code>WebElement::getText</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Default methods</strong> — add helpers to listener interfaces without breaking implementers (this is why you can override just <code>onTestFailure</code> in ITestListener).</li>\n<li><strong>var</strong> (Java 10+), <strong>text blocks</strong> (Java 15+) — very handy for inline JSON payloads.</li>\n</ul>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Why are Strings immutable, and what is the String pool?","a":"\n<p>String objects can't be modified after creation — every \"change\" creates a new object. Reasons: safe sharing across threads, hashcode caching (so Strings work well as HashMap keys), and security (a file path or URL can't be mutated after validation).</p>\n<p>The <strong>string pool</strong> keeps literals in a shared area, so <code>\"abc\" == \"abc\"</code> is true but <code>new String(\"abc\") == \"abc\"</code> is false — always compare with <code>.equals()</code>. Use <code>StringBuilder</code> when concatenating in loops.</p>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Explain equals() and hashCode() — why does the contract matter?","a":"\n<p>Contract: equal objects must return equal hash codes. Break it and HashMap/HashSet stop working — two \"equal\" objects land in different buckets, so lookups miss and duplicates slip in.</p>\n<p>In automation this bites when you build a POJO for API response comparison: if you override <code>equals()</code> to compare fields but forget <code>hashCode()</code>, your set-based duplicate check silently passes.</p>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Abstract class vs interface — when do you use which?","a":"\n<table>\n<tr><th>Abstract class</th><th>Interface</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Concrete + abstract methods</td><td>Abstract by default (default/static allowed since Java 8)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Single inheritance</td><td>Implement many</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Constructors, instance state</td><td>Constants only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"is-a\" with shared code — BasePage</td><td>Contract/capability — WebDriver, ITestListener</td></tr>\n</table>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>One-liner</b>Abstract class when pages share behaviour; interface when unrelated classes must honour the same contract.</div>"},
{"c":"java","q":"String vs StringBuilder vs StringBuffer?","a":"\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>String</th><th>StringBuilder</th><th>StringBuffer</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Mutable</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Thread-safe</td><td>Yes (immutable)</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (synchronized)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed</td><td>Slow for concatenation</td><td>Fastest</td><td>Slower than Builder</td></tr>\n</table>\n<pre><code>String s = \"\";\nfor (WebElement e : rows) s += e.getText();      // creates a new object each loop\n\nStringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\nfor (WebElement e : rows) sb.append(e.getText()); // one object, mutated\nString result = sb.toString();</code></pre>\n<p>Default to StringBuilder inside loops; StringBuffer only when the same buffer is shared across threads — rare in test code.</p>"},
{"c":"java","q":"int vs Integer — and what are wrapper classes?","a":"\n<p><code>int</code> is a primitive (8 bytes of stack value, can't be null). <code>Integer</code> is the wrapper <strong>object</strong> — it can be null, carries methods like <code>parseInt()</code>, and is required by collections and generics (<code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code>, not <code>List&lt;int&gt;</code>).</p>\n<p>Wrapper classes: Byte, Short, Integer, Long, Float, Double, Character, Boolean. <strong>Autoboxing</strong> converts primitive → wrapper automatically, unboxing goes the other way.</p>\n<pre><code>Integer a = 127, b = 127;\nSystem.out.println(a == b);        // true  — cached range -128..127\n\nInteger x = 128, y = 128;\nSystem.out.println(x == y);        // false — different objects!\nSystem.out.println(x.equals(y));   // true  — always compare with equals</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Gotcha</b>Unboxing a null Integer throws NullPointerException — common when a JSON field is missing and you assign it to an int.</div>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Difference between == and .equals()?","a":"\n<p><code>==</code> compares <strong>references</strong> for objects (and values for primitives). <code>.equals()</code> compares <strong>content</strong>, as overridden by the class.</p>\n<pre><code>String a = \"chrome\";\nString b = \"chrome\";\nString c = new String(\"chrome\");\n\na == b;         // true  — both point to the same pooled literal\na == c;         // false — c is a new object on the heap\na.equals(c);    // true  — same characters</code></pre>\n<p>In tests this matters constantly: <code>driver.getTitle() == \"Dashboard\"</code> can be false even when the text matches. Always use <code>.equals()</code>, or better, an assertion library: <code>assertEquals(driver.getTitle(), \"Dashboard\")</code>.</p>"},
{"c":"java","q":"ArrayList vs LinkedList vs Vector — which do you use?","a":"\n<table>\n<tr><th>ArrayList</th><th>LinkedList</th><th>Vector</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Dynamic array, index access O(1)</td><td>Doubly linked nodes, access O(n)</td><td>Legacy, synchronized ArrayList</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Slower insert/delete in the middle</td><td>Fast insert/delete</td><td>Slow — locks on every call</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Default choice for automation</td><td>Queue/stack-style workloads</td><td>Avoid; use CopyOnWriteArrayList if you truly need thread safety</td></tr>\n</table>\n<pre><code>List&lt;WebElement&gt; rows = driver.findElements(By.cssSelector(\".item\"));  // ArrayList\nList&lt;String&gt; texts = rows.stream().map(WebElement::getText).toList();</code></pre>\n<p><code>findElements()</code> returns a List — an empty list, not an exception, when nothing matches. That's the standard way to assert an element is absent.</p>"},
{"c":"java","q":"HashMap vs Hashtable vs ConcurrentHashMap?","a":"\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>HashMap</th><th>Hashtable</th><th>ConcurrentHashMap</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Thread-safe</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (whole-map lock)</td><td>Yes (segment/bucket level)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Null key/value</td><td>One null key, many null values</td><td>None allowed</td><td>None allowed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Use</td><td>Test data, headers, locator maps</td><td>Legacy — avoid</td><td>Shared state across parallel threads</td></tr>\n</table>\n<pre><code>Map&lt;String, String&gt; headers = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();\nheaders.put(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\");\nheaders.put(\"Authorization\", \"Bearer \" + token);\n\n// TreeMap when you need keys sorted, LinkedHashMap to preserve insertion order</code></pre>"},
{"c":"java","q":"throw vs throws — and how do try/catch/finally fit in?","a":"\n<p><code>throw</code> raises an exception at a point in code. <code>throws</code> declares in the method signature that it may propagate one, pushing responsibility to the caller.</p>\n<pre><code>public String readConfig(String path) throws IOException {   // declares\n    if (path == null) throw new FrameworkException(\"Config path missing\");  // raises\n    return Files.readString(Path.of(path));\n}\n\ntry {\n    readConfig(null);\n} catch (FrameworkException | IOException e) {\n    logger.error(\"Config load failed\", e);\n    throw new SkipException(\"Environment not configured\");\n} finally {\n    driver.quit();      // always runs, pass or fail\n}</code></pre>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Explain access modifiers and where they show up in a framework.","a":"\n<table>\n<tr><th>Modifier</th><th>Same class</th><th>Same package</th><th>Subclass</th><th>Everywhere</th></tr>\n<tr><td>private</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>default (no keyword)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>protected</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>public</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n</table>\n<pre><code>public class LoginPage extends BasePage {\n    private By usernameField = By.id(\"username\");   // locators stay private\n    protected WebDriver driver;                     // shared with subclasses\n    public void login(String u, String p) { }       // the only thing tests call\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Note: classes themselves can only be <code>public</code> or default.</p>"},
{"c":"java","q":"What do final, static and abstract mean (non-access modifiers)?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>final</strong> — a class can't be extended, a method can't be overridden, a variable can't be reassigned. Use for constants: <code>private static final int TIMEOUT = 30;</code></li>\n<li><strong>static</strong> — belongs to the class, not an instance. Utility methods (<code>ScreenshotUtil.capture()</code>) and constants. Careful: static mutable state breaks parallel execution.</li>\n<li><strong>abstract</strong> — a class that can't be instantiated, or a method with no body that subclasses must implement.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>public abstract class BasePage {\n    protected static final Duration TIMEOUT = Duration.ofSeconds(15);\n    public abstract boolean isPageLoaded();   // no body — every page defines it\n}</code></pre>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Explain constructors — default, parameterized, and super().","a":"\n<p>A constructor initialises an object, shares the class name, and has no return type. If you don't write one, Java supplies a no-arg default; the moment you write any constructor, that default disappears.</p>\n<pre><code>public class BasePage {\n    protected WebDriver driver;\n    public BasePage(WebDriver driver) { this.driver = driver; }\n}\n\npublic class LoginPage extends BasePage {\n    public LoginPage(WebDriver driver) {\n        super(driver);        // must be the first statement — builds the parent first\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>This is exactly why every page object takes the driver in its constructor: the page can't do anything without it, so the compiler enforces it.</p>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Can an interface have variables?","a":"\n<p>Yes, but only <strong>constants</strong> — every field is implicitly <code>public static final</code>, so it must be initialised and can never be reassigned. Interfaces hold no instance state.</p>\n<pre><code>public interface TestConstants {\n    int DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 30;          // implicitly public static final\n    String BASE_URL = \"https://qa.app.com\";\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Since Java 8 interfaces can also have <code>default</code> and <code>static</code> methods (that's how TestNG lets you override only <code>onTestFailure</code>), and since Java 9, <code>private</code> helper methods.</p>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Method overloading vs overriding?","a":"\n<table>\n<tr><th>Overloading (compile-time)</th><th>Overriding (runtime)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Same name, different parameters</td><td>Same signature in a subclass</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Resolved by the compiler</td><td>Resolved by the JVM at runtime</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Same class</td><td>Requires inheritance</td></tr>\n</table>\n<pre><code>// Overloading\nprotected void click(By locator) { }\nprotected void click(By locator, int timeoutSeconds) { }\n\n// Overriding\npublic class CheckoutPage extends BasePage {\n    @Override\n    protected void click(By locator) {          // JS click for this stubborn page\n        ((JavascriptExecutor) driver).executeScript(\n            \"arguments[0].click();\", driver.findElement(locator));\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Runtime polymorphism is what makes <code>WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();</code> work — the reference type is the interface, the behaviour comes from the object.</p>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Which Java version do you use, and what do you use from it?","a":"\n<p>Java 17 is the current widely-adopted LTS (Java 21 is the next). Features that actually change test code:</p>\n<pre><code>// Text blocks (15+) — inline JSON payloads without escaping\nString payload = \"\"\"\n    { \"name\": \"Karthik\", \"role\": \"QA Lead\" }\n    \"\"\";\n\n// var (10+)\nvar wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(15));\n\n// Records (16+) — perfect for API response POJOs\npublic record User(String id, String name, String role) {}\n\n// Switch expressions (14+)\nWebDriver driver = switch (browser) {\n    case \"chrome\"  -&gt; new ChromeDriver();\n    case \"firefox\" -&gt; new FirefoxDriver();\n    default -&gt; throw new FrameworkException(\"Unsupported browser: \" + browser);\n};</code></pre>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"Where have you used OOPS concepts in Selenium?","a":"\n<p>All five pillars show up in a Page Object framework — that's what makes it modular, reusable and maintainable. Walk the interviewer through them in this order.</p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Class and Object</strong> — one class per page (LoginPage, HomePage, CartPage); test classes create objects of them and call their methods.</p>\n<pre><code>public class LoginPage {\n    WebDriver driver;\n\n    public LoginPage(WebDriver driver) {\n        this.driver = driver;\n    }\n\n    public void login(String username, String password) {\n        driver.findElement(By.id(\"username\")).sendKeys(username);\n        driver.findElement(By.id(\"password\")).sendKeys(password);\n        driver.findElement(By.id(\"login\")).click();\n    }\n}\n\n// In the test class\nLoginPage loginPage = new LoginPage(driver);\nloginPage.login(\"admin\", \"admin123\");</code></pre>\n<p><em>Why:</em> organises the code by page and lets the same page object serve many test cases.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. Inheritance</strong> — a BaseTest holds the setup and teardown every test needs; each test class extends it.</p>\n<pre><code>public class BaseTest {\n    WebDriver driver;\n\n    @BeforeMethod\n    public void setUp() { driver = new ChromeDriver(); }\n\n    @AfterMethod\n    public void tearDown() { driver.quit(); }\n}\n\npublic class LoginTest extends BaseTest {\n    @Test\n    public void verifyLogin() {\n        new LoginPage(driver).login(\"admin\", \"admin123\");\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p><em>Why:</em> removes duplicate setup code across 200 test classes and gives you one place to change it.</p>\n\n<p><strong>3. Encapsulation</strong> — locators and helpers stay private inside the page class; tests only touch public business methods.</p>\n<pre><code>public class LoginPage {\n    private WebDriver driver;\n    private By usernameField = By.id(\"username\");\n\n    public LoginPage(WebDriver driver) { this.driver = driver; }\n\n    public void enterUsername(String username) {\n        driver.findElement(usernameField).sendKeys(username);\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p><em>Why:</em> hides implementation detail, so a locator change is a one-file fix and no test depends directly on the DOM.</p>\n\n<p><strong>4. Polymorphism</strong> — every browser driver implements the same WebDriver interface (runtime), and you overload helper methods for different signatures (compile time).</p>\n<pre><code>// Runtime — same reference type, different behaviour\nWebDriver driver;\ndriver = new ChromeDriver();     // or\ndriver = new FirefoxDriver();\n\n// Compile time — overloading\nprotected void click(By locator) { }\nprotected void click(By locator, int timeoutSeconds) { }</code></pre>\n<p><em>Why:</em> the same test code runs cross-browser with no changes beyond a config value.</p>\n\n<p><strong>5. Abstraction</strong> — utility and base methods hide the mechanics so tests read as business intent.</p>\n<pre><code>// Instead of repeating this everywhere\ndriver.findElement(By.id(\"submit\")).click();\n\n// The base class exposes\npublic void clickElement(By locator) {\n    wait.until(ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(locator)).click();\n}\n\n// And the test simply says\nclickElement(submitButton);</code></pre>\n<p><em>Why:</em> keeps test scripts clean and centralises the wait/retry logic — improve it once, every test benefits.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Don't just recite the four pillars. Name the file each one lives in, and the maintenance problem it solved — that's what separates someone who has built a framework from someone who has read about one.</div>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"How do you handle dynamic elements and synchronisation in Selenium?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Explicit waits</strong> — <code>WebDriverWait</code> + <code>ExpectedConditions</code> for the specific state you need.</li>\n<li><strong>FluentWait</strong> — custom polling interval and ignored exceptions for slow, jittery components.</li>\n<li><strong>Stable locators</strong> — relative XPath on stable attributes (<code>data-testid</code>), never absolute paths or index-based selectors.</li>\n<li><strong>App-aware waits</strong> — wait for the spinner to disappear or the AJAX call to settle, wrapped in a reusable utility.</li>\n<li><strong>Never</strong> <code>Thread.sleep()</code> — it's either too short (flaky) or too long (slow suite).</li>\n</ul>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"Abstraction and interface in Selenium — give real examples.","a":"\n<p><strong>1. WebDriver is the textbook interface.</strong> ChromeDriver/FirefoxDriver implement it, so switching browsers costs zero test changes:</p>\n<pre><code>WebDriver driver = browser.equals(\"chrome\")\n        ? new ChromeDriver()\n        : new FirefoxDriver();\ndriver.get(\"https://example.com\");</code></pre>\n<p>You call <code>get()</code> without knowing how each browser implements it — that's abstraction.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Custom interface for a swappable reporter:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>public interface ITestReporter {\n    void logStep(String message);\n    void logFailure(String message, String screenshotPath);\n}\n// ExtentReporter and AllureReporter implement it —\n// tests depend on the interface, so you swap tools without touching tests.</code></pre>\n<p><strong>3. Abstract class for BasePage</strong> — shared implemented helpers plus a contract every page must honour:</p>\n<pre><code>public abstract class BasePage {\n    protected WebDriver driver;\n    protected WebDriverWait wait;\n\n    public BasePage(WebDriver driver) {\n        this.driver = driver;\n        this.wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));\n    }\n\n    protected void click(By locator) {\n        wait.until(ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(locator)).click();\n    }\n\n    public abstract boolean isPageLoaded();   // every page must define this\n}</code></pre>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"How do you run Selenium tests in parallel safely?","a":"\n<p>TestNG handles the threading, your framework handles the isolation:</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;suite name=\"Regression\" parallel=\"methods\" thread-count=\"4\"&gt;</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>ThreadLocal WebDriver — no shared driver, no static mutable state.</li>\n<li>Independent test data per thread (unique IDs), no test depending on another's output.</li>\n<li>Scale out with Selenium Grid / Selenoid / cloud providers rather than pushing thread count on one box.</li>\n<li>Thread-safe reporting — ExtentTest also needs ThreadLocal.</li>\n</ul>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"Explain the Page Object Model and PageFactory. Any downsides?","a":"\n<p>POM keeps locators and page interactions in one class per page, so a UI change is a one-file fix. Methods should express business intent (<code>login()</code>), not mechanics (<code>clickButton()</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>PageFactory</strong> (<code>@FindBy</code> + <code>initElements</code>) reduces boilerplate through lazy proxies, but it's effectively deprecated in modern practice — it hides staleness issues and doesn't play well with dynamic SPAs. Many teams now prefer plain <code>By</code> locators.</p>\n<p><strong>Downsides of POM done badly:</strong> giant god-pages, assertions leaking into page classes (keep them in tests), and deep inheritance chains that nobody can trace.</p>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"How do you handle windows, frames, alerts and shadow DOM?","a":"\n<pre><code>// Windows\nString parent = driver.getWindowHandle();\nfor (String h : driver.getWindowHandles())\n    if (!h.equals(parent)) driver.switchTo().window(h);\n\n// Frames\ndriver.switchTo().frame(\"frameName\");\ndriver.switchTo().defaultContent();\n\n// Alerts\nAlert alert = driver.switchTo().alert();\nalert.accept();\n\n// Shadow DOM (Selenium 4+)\nWebElement root = driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"my-widget\"));\nroot.getShadowRoot().findElement(By.cssSelector(\"input\")).sendKeys(\"test\");</code></pre>\n<p>Always switch back to <code>defaultContent()</code> after frame work, and wait for the frame/alert to be present rather than assuming.</p>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"What's new in Selenium 4 that you actually use?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>W3C protocol</strong> by default — fewer cross-browser quirks.</li>\n<li><strong>Relative locators</strong> — <code>with(By.tagName(\"input\")).toRightOf(label)</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>CDP access</strong> — network mocking, geolocation, console log capture, basic auth.</li>\n<li><strong>Native shadow DOM support</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Full-page screenshot / element screenshot</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Duration-based waits</strong> and cleaner Grid 4 (router/distributor/node, Docker-friendly).</li>\n</ul>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"How do you generate customer/test data for Selenium tests?","a":"\n<p>Four sources, in order of preference:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>API creation</strong> — the fastest and most reliable: create the customer through the service layer in setup, then drive the UI.</li>\n<li><strong>Faker</strong> — realistic random values for names, emails, addresses.</li>\n<li><strong>DB queries</strong> — pick an existing record matching required criteria (funded account, active policy).</li>\n<li><strong>External files</strong> — JSON/Excel for fixed business scenarios.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>Faker faker = new Faker();\nString email = faker.internet().emailAddress();      // unique-ish\nString uniqueEmail = \"qa+\" + System.currentTimeMillis() + \"@test.com\"; // guaranteed\n\n// Create via API, then verify in UI\nString customerId = given().spec(authSpec)\n        .body(new Customer(faker.name().firstName(), uniqueEmail))\n        .post(\"/customers\").jsonPath().getString(\"id\");\n\ndriver.get(BASE_URL + \"/customers/\" + customerId);</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Never depend on data someone created manually last quarter — a test that needs a hand-made record is a test that will fail in a fresh environment.</div>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"What's your locator strategy and why?","a":"\n<p>Order of preference: <strong>ID → name → CSS selector → XPath</strong>, and relative locators when the DOM gives you nothing stable.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ID</strong> — fastest and most stable, if the devs provide it.</li>\n<li><strong>CSS</strong> — concise, fast, good for class/attribute combinations.</li>\n<li><strong>XPath</strong> — the only option for traversing up the tree or matching text; slowest and most brittle, so keep it relative.</li>\n<li><strong>Link text / partial link text</strong> — fine for stable navigation links, breaks with copy changes or localisation.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>By.cssSelector(\"input[data-testid='username']\");\nBy.xpath(\"//tr[td[text()='Karthik']]//button[@title='Edit']\");\n\n// Selenium 4 relative locators\nBy.name(\"password\") // or:\nRelativeLocator.with(By.tagName(\"input\")).below(By.id(\"username\"));</code></pre>\n<p>Validate in DevTools console before coding: <code>$$(\"css\")</code> and <code>$x(\"//xpath\")</code>. Never use absolute XPath or index-based paths — one new div breaks 40 tests.</p>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"Implicit vs explicit vs fluent wait — and why not mix them?","a":"\n<pre><code>// Implicit — global, applies to every findElement, set once\ndriver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(10));\n\n// Explicit — condition-specific, the one you should use\nnew WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(15))\n    .until(ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(By.id(\"submit\")));\n\n// Fluent — explicit wait with tuned polling and ignored exceptions\nWait&lt;WebDriver&gt; wait = new FluentWait&lt;&gt;(driver)\n    .withTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))\n    .pollingEvery(Duration.ofMillis(500))\n    .ignoring(NoSuchElementException.class);</code></pre>\n<p>Common ExpectedConditions: <code>visibilityOfElementLocated</code>, <code>presenceOfElementLocated</code>, <code>elementToBeClickable</code>, <code>invisibilityOfElementLocated</code>, <code>alertIsPresent</code>, <code>titleContains</code>, <code>frameToBeAvailableAndSwitchToIt</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Why not mix</b>Combining implicit and explicit waits produces unpredictable timeouts — the two mechanisms compound. Pick explicit waits and set implicit to zero.</div>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"Where do you use a Set in Selenium?","a":"\n<p>Window and tab handles — they're unique by definition, so Selenium returns a Set:</p>\n<pre><code>String parent = driver.getWindowHandle();\nSet&lt;String&gt; handles = driver.getWindowHandles();\n\nfor (String handle : handles) {\n    if (!handle.equals(parent)) {\n        driver.switchTo().window(handle);\n        break;\n    }\n}\n// ... work in the new window\ndriver.close();\ndriver.switchTo().window(parent);</code></pre>\n<p>The other common use is duplicate detection in table or API data: put a List into a HashSet and compare sizes.</p>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"What is the Actions class and when do you need it?","a":"\n<p>Actions builds a chain of low-level user interactions — hover, drag, right-click, keyboard combinations — that plain <code>click()</code> and <code>sendKeys()</code> can't express.</p>\n<pre><code>Actions actions = new Actions(driver);\n\nactions.moveToElement(menu)                  // hover to open a dropdown\n       .click(submenuItem)\n       .build().perform();\n\nactions.dragAndDrop(source, target).perform();\nactions.contextClick(row).perform();          // right-click\nactions.keyDown(Keys.CONTROL).sendKeys(\"a\").keyUp(Keys.CONTROL).perform();\nactions.scrollToElement(footer).perform();    // Selenium 4.2+</code></pre>\n<p>Always end with <code>perform()</code> — <code>build()</code> alone just composes the action.</p>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"When do you use JavascriptExecutor?","a":"\n<pre><code>JavascriptExecutor js = (JavascriptExecutor) driver;\n\njs.executeScript(\"arguments[0].scrollIntoView(true);\", element);\njs.executeScript(\"arguments[0].click();\", element);        // element covered by an overlay\njs.executeScript(\"arguments[0].value='QA';\", inputField);  // bypasses keystrokes\nString state = (String) js.executeScript(\"return document.readyState;\");</code></pre>\n<p>Legitimate uses: scrolling, reading page state, working around a sticky header that intercepts clicks, and generating screenshots of long pages.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Caution</b>A JS click bypasses what a real user experiences — it will happily \"click\" a disabled or invisible button and give you a false pass. Treat it as a workaround, and log why it was needed.</div>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"How do you keep page objects maintainable as the app grows?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One class per page or major component</strong> — a shared header/nav/modal becomes its own component class reused by many pages.</li>\n<li><strong>No assertions in page objects</strong> — pages return data or another page; tests assert.</li>\n<li><strong>Return types express flow</strong> — <code>login()</code> returns a DashboardPage, so navigation is compile-time checked.</li>\n<li><strong>Locators in one place per page</strong>, private, named after intent not markup.</li>\n<li><strong>Standards enforced in PR review</strong> — naming, no waits in tests, no driver calls outside pages.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>public DashboardPage login(String user, String pass) {\n    type(usernameField, user);\n    type(passwordField, pass);\n    click(loginButton);\n    return new DashboardPage(driver);\n}</code></pre>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"Which Selenium exceptions do you hit most, and how do you handle them?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NoSuchElementException</strong> — locator wrong or element not rendered yet. Fix with an explicit wait, not a retry.</li>\n<li><strong>StaleElementReferenceException</strong> — the DOM re-rendered after you found the element. Re-find inside the wrapper method.</li>\n<li><strong>ElementClickInterceptedException</strong> — an overlay, cookie banner or sticky header is on top. Scroll, dismiss the banner, or JS-click.</li>\n<li><strong>ElementNotInteractableException</strong> — present but hidden/disabled. Wait for <code>elementToBeClickable</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>TimeoutException</strong> — the condition never became true; usually a genuine app bug or a wrong expectation.</li>\n<li><strong>InvalidSelectorException</strong> — malformed XPath/CSS syntax.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>protected void safeClick(By locator) {\n    try {\n        wait.until(ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(locator)).click();\n    } catch (StaleElementReferenceException e) {\n        driver.findElement(locator).click();   // re-find once, then let it fail\n    }\n}</code></pre>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"How do you wire ExtentReports into the framework?","a":"\n<p>Reports are driven by listeners, never by test code, and the report object is thread-local for parallel runs.</p>\n<pre><code>public class ExtentManager {\n    private static ExtentReports extent;\n    private static ThreadLocal&lt;ExtentTest&gt; test = new ThreadLocal&lt;&gt;();\n\n    public static ExtentTest get() { return test.get(); }\n}\n\npublic class TestListener implements ITestListener {\n    @Override public void onTestFailure(ITestResult result) {\n        ExtentManager.get()\n            .log(Status.FAIL, result.getThrowable())\n            .addScreenCaptureFromPath(ScreenshotUtil.capture(result.getName()));\n    }\n    @Override public void onTestSuccess(ITestResult result) {\n        ExtentManager.get().log(Status.PASS, \"Passed\");\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>ExtentReports is the report; ExtentTest is one test node inside it. Flush the report in <code>@AfterSuite</code> or the HTML never gets written.</p>"},
{"c":"selenium","q":"How do you use Selenium Grid?","a":"\n<p>Grid separates where tests run from where they're written — one hub distributes sessions to nodes with different browsers and OS versions.</p>\n<pre><code>ChromeOptions options = new ChromeOptions();\noptions.setPlatformName(\"Linux\");\n\nWebDriver driver = new RemoteWebDriver(\n        new URL(\"http://grid-hub:4444/wd/hub\"), options);</code></pre>\n<p>Grid 4 splits into router, distributor, session map and nodes, and runs cleanly in Docker (or Selenoid) so CI agents are identical to local. Combine <code>parallel=\"methods\"</code> in testng.xml with enough nodes — thread count beyond node capacity just queues.</p>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"What makes Playwright different from Selenium?","a":"\n<p>Playwright talks to the browser over DevTools protocol rather than the WebDriver HTTP protocol — fewer hops, less flakiness. Practical differences:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Auto-waiting</strong> baked into every action.</li>\n<li><strong>Browser contexts</strong> — cheap isolation, parallel sessions in one browser.</li>\n<li><strong>Network interception</strong> and API mocking out of the box.</li>\n<li><strong>Built-in test runner</strong> with parallelism, retries, fixtures, reporters.</li>\n<li><strong>Trace Viewer</strong> — time-travel debugging with DOM snapshots.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Selenium still wins on language/browser breadth and existing enterprise Grid infrastructure.</p>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"Explain auto-waiting. Do you ever still need explicit waits?","a":"\n<p>Before every action Playwright waits for the element to be attached, visible, stable, enabled and able to receive events — up to the timeout. So <code>click()</code> rarely needs a preceding wait.</p>\n<p>Explicit waits remain useful for non-action conditions:</p>\n<pre><code>await expect(locator).toBeVisible();        // auto-retrying assertion\nawait page.waitForResponse('**/api/users'); // network\nawait page.waitForURL('**/dashboard');      // navigation</code></pre>\n<p><code>page.waitForTimeout()</code> is the Thread.sleep of Playwright — debugging only.</p>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"What are browser contexts and how do you reuse login state?","a":"\n<p>A BrowserContext is an isolated incognito-like session inside one browser — separate cookies, storage and cache. It gives you parallel isolation cheaply and lets you run two users in one test (admin + customer).</p>\n<pre><code>// global setup: log in once\nawait context.storageState({ path: 'auth.json' });\n\n// every test starts logged in\nconst context = await browser.newContext({ storageState: 'auth.json' });</code></pre>\n<p>Skipping the login UI in every test is often the single biggest suite speed-up.</p>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"How do Playwright locators work and what's the recommended strategy?","a":"\n<p>Locators are <strong>lazy</strong> — resolved at action time, so they survive re-renders and stale element errors mostly disappear.</p>\n<pre><code>page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Submit' })\npage.getByLabel('Email')\npage.getByText('Welcome back')\npage.getByTestId('checkout-btn')\n\n// chaining + filtering\npage.getByRole('row').filter({ hasText: 'Karthik' }).getByRole('button')</code></pre>\n<p>Priority: role/label/text (user-facing, survives refactors) → test id → CSS → XPath as last resort.</p>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"How do you mock or intercept network calls?","a":"\n<pre><code>await page.route('**/api/products', route =&gt;\n  route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify(mockData) })\n);\n\n// block third-party noise to speed up runs\nawait page.route('**/*analytics*', route =&gt; route.abort());</code></pre>\n<p>Use it to force edge cases (empty state, 500 error, slow response) without backend setup, and <code>waitForResponse()</code> to spy on real calls and assert payloads.</p>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"How do parallelism and retries work in Playwright Test?","a":"\n<p>Each test <em>file</em> runs in its own worker process by default; <code>fullyParallel: true</code> parallelises tests within a file too. Config drives it:</p>\n<pre><code>export default defineConfig({\n  workers: process.env.CI ? 4 : undefined,\n  fullyParallel: true,\n  retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0,\n  reporter: [['html'], ['junit', { outputFile: 'results.xml' }]],\n});</code></pre>\n<p>A test that passes on retry is reported as <strong>flaky</strong> — a first-class signal you can track. Split across CI machines with <code>--shard=1/4</code>.</p>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"How do you debug a failing Playwright test?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trace Viewer</strong> — <code>trace: 'on-first-retry'</code>, then <code>npx playwright show-trace</code>: full timeline, DOM snapshots, network, console.</li>\n<li><strong>UI Mode</strong> — <code>npx playwright test --ui</code> for time-travel + watch mode.</li>\n<li><strong>Inspector</strong> — <code>--debug</code> to step through actions and pick locators.</li>\n<li><strong>Artifacts</strong> — <code>screenshot: 'only-on-failure'</code>, <code>video: 'retain-on-failure'</code>, published from CI.</li>\n</ul>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"What are fixtures, and how do they beat beforeEach hooks?","a":"\n<pre><code>export const test = base.extend({\n  loggedInPage: async ({ page }, use) =&gt; {\n    await page.goto('/login');\n    await loginAs(page, 'admin');\n    await use(page);       // test body runs here\n    await logout(page);    // teardown\n  },\n});</code></pre>\n<p>Fixtures are <strong>lazy</strong> (only run when a test asks for them), composable, and shareable across projects. Hooks run for every test in the file whether needed or not, and don't compose.</p>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"How would you migrate a large Selenium suite to Playwright?","a":"\n<p>Don't rewrite line-for-line, and don't stop the world:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pilot</strong> — migrate one high-value, high-flake suite first and measure runtime + stability against Selenium.</li>\n<li><strong>Run in parallel</strong> for a release or two; Playwright is advisory until it proves stable.</li>\n<li><strong>Rebuild, don't port</strong> — drop explicit waits, replace XPath with role/testid locators, replace UI login with storageState.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep the layer boundaries</strong> — page objects and test data utilities usually survive the move conceptually.</li>\n<li><strong>Skill the team</strong> — JS/TS ramp-up is the real cost, not the API.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Have a stop condition: if the pilot doesn't beat the old suite on runtime and flake rate, you don't migrate.</div>"},
{"c":"cucumber","q":"Scenario vs Scenario Outline?","a":"\n<p>A <strong>Scenario</strong> runs once. A <strong>Scenario Outline</strong> is a template run once per row of its Examples table — the BDD equivalent of a DataProvider.</p>\n<pre><code>Scenario: Valid login\n  Given the user is on the login page\n  When the user logs in with \"admin\" and \"admin123\"\n  Then the dashboard is displayed\n\nScenario Outline: Login validation\n  Given the user is on the login page\n  When the user logs in with \"&lt;username&gt;\" and \"&lt;password&gt;\"\n  Then the message \"&lt;message&gt;\" is displayed\n\n  Examples:\n    | username | password  | message              |\n    | admin    | wrongpass | Invalid credentials  |\n    |          | admin123  | Username is required |</code></pre>"},
{"c":"cucumber","q":"How do you pass test data in Cucumber?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inline parameters</strong> in the step text — simplest.</li>\n<li><strong>Examples table</strong> with Scenario Outline — same steps, many data sets.</li>\n<li><strong>DataTable</strong> — structured data for one step.</li>\n<li><strong>External source</strong> — the feature carries a key, the step definition looks the data up in JSON/Excel/DB. Best for large or sensitive data.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>When the user registers with the following details\n  | firstName | lastName | email             |\n  | Karthik   | S        | qa@test.com       |</code></pre>\n<pre><code>@When(\"the user registers with the following details\")\npublic void register(DataTable table) {\n    List&lt;Map&lt;String, String&gt;&gt; rows = table.asMaps(String.class, String.class);\n    registerPage.register(rows.get(0).get(\"firstName\"), rows.get(0).get(\"email\"));\n}</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Keep it readable</b>Feature files are for business readers. Passwords, tokens and 20-column tables belong in a data file, not in Gherkin.</div>"},
{"c":"cucumber","q":"How is a Cucumber project structured?","a":"\n<pre><code>src/test/resources/features/login.feature       # Gherkin\nsrc/test/java/stepdefs/LoginSteps.java          # glue code\nsrc/test/java/hooks/Hooks.java                  # @Before / @After\nsrc/test/java/runners/TestRunner.java           # entry point</code></pre>\n<pre><code>@CucumberOptions(\n    features = \"src/test/resources/features\",\n    glue     = {\"stepdefs\", \"hooks\"},\n    tags     = \"@smoke and not @wip\",\n    plugin   = {\"pretty\", \"html:target/cucumber.html\",\n                \"json:target/cucumber.json\"},\n    monochrome = true\n)\npublic class TestRunner extends AbstractTestNGCucumberTests {\n    @Override\n    @DataProvider(parallel = true)\n    public Object[][] scenarios() { return super.scenarios(); }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Step definitions stay thin — they call page objects, they don't contain locators or waits.</p>"},
{"c":"cucumber","q":"Background vs Hooks (@Before / @After)?","a":"\n<p><strong>Background</strong> is Gherkin — business steps repeated before every scenario in a feature, visible to readers. <strong>Hooks</strong> are Java — technical setup and teardown that shouldn't clutter the feature file.</p>\n<pre><code>Background:\n  Given the user is logged in as an administrator</code></pre>\n<pre><code>public class Hooks {\n    @Before(\"@ui\")\n    public void setUp() { DriverFactory.set(new ChromeDriver()); }\n\n    @After\n    public void tearDown(Scenario scenario) {\n        if (scenario.isFailed()) {\n            byte[] shot = ((TakesScreenshot) DriverFactory.get())\n                    .getScreenshotAs(OutputType.BYTES);\n            scenario.attach(shot, \"image/png\", scenario.getName());\n        }\n        DriverFactory.unload();\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Rule: if a business stakeholder would care about it, it's a Background step; if it's driver or database plumbing, it's a hook.</p>"},
{"c":"cucumber","q":"Is BDD worth it? When would you not use Cucumber?","a":"\n<p>BDD pays off when business analysts and developers genuinely write and read the scenarios — shared language, living documentation, requirements clarified before coding.</p>\n<p>It costs you an extra glue layer, slower authoring, and a maintenance burden nobody mentions upfront. If only QA ever opens the feature files, you've paid the cost for none of the benefit — plain TestNG or JUnit tests will be faster to write and easier to debug.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>The honest answer in an interview: I've used Cucumber where the three amigos process was real, and dropped it where the feature files had become a translation layer written by testers, for testers.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"What is an API, and what makes up a request and a response?","a":"\n<p>An API is a contract between two systems — the client sends a structured request, the server returns a structured response. Everything you test lives in one of those two envelopes.</p>\n<pre><code>REQUEST\n  Method + URL   POST https://api.app.com/v1/bookings\n  Headers        Content-Type, Authorization, Accept\n  Params         path (/bookings/101) and query (?status=ACTIVE)\n  Body           JSON payload (POST/PUT/PATCH only)\n\nRESPONSE\n  Status line    HTTP/1.1 201 Created\n  Headers        Content-Type, Location, ETag, rate-limit info\n  Body           JSON — the created/requested resource, or an error object\n  Timing         how long the round trip took</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Framing</b>Every API test case is just: \"given this request, what should come back in each of those four parts?\" If you can articulate it that way, the test cases write themselves.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"Walk me through the HTTP methods and what each is for.","a":"\n<table>\n<tr><th>Method</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Body</th><th>Typical success code</th></tr>\n<tr><td>GET</td><td>Read a resource or collection</td><td>No</td><td>200 OK</td></tr>\n<tr><td>POST</td><td>Create a new resource, or trigger an action</td><td>Yes</td><td>201 Created / 200</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PUT</td><td>Replace a resource entirely</td><td>Yes</td><td>200 / 204</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PATCH</td><td>Partially update a resource</td><td>Yes</td><td>200 / 204</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DELETE</td><td>Remove a resource</td><td>Usually no</td><td>204 No Content / 200</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HEAD</td><td>Same as GET but headers only — existence and size checks</td><td>No</td><td>200</td></tr>\n<tr><td>OPTIONS</td><td>Which methods the endpoint allows; CORS preflight</td><td>No</td><td>200 / 204</td></tr>\n</table>\n<pre><code>GET    /bookings?firstname=Karthik   # filter a collection\nGET    /bookings/101                 # one resource\nPOST   /bookings                     # create -> returns 201 + Location header\nPUT    /bookings/101                 # full replace\nPATCH  /bookings/101                 # change one field\nDELETE /bookings/101                 # remove</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Say this</b>The method is a contract, not a preference. An API that creates records on a GET has a design defect worth raising, regardless of whether it works.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"PUT vs PATCH — and how do you test the difference?","a":"\n<p><strong>PUT replaces the whole resource.</strong> Any field you omit is typically reset to null or its default. <strong>PATCH updates only what you send</strong>; everything else stays untouched.</p>\n<pre><code>// Existing record\n{ \"firstname\": \"Karthik\", \"lastname\": \"S\", \"totalprice\": 500, \"deposit\": true }\n\n// PUT with a partial body — the omitted fields are wiped\nPUT /booking/101\n{ \"firstname\": \"Karthik\" }\n-&gt; { \"firstname\": \"Karthik\", \"lastname\": null, \"totalprice\": null, \"deposit\": null }\n\n// PATCH with the same body — only firstname changes\nPATCH /booking/101\n{ \"firstname\": \"Karthik\" }\n-&gt; { \"firstname\": \"Karthik\", \"lastname\": \"S\", \"totalprice\": 500, \"deposit\": true }</code></pre>\n<p><strong>The test:</strong> GET the record first, send a partial PUT, GET again and assert the untouched fields. If they survived, the API is behaving like PATCH under a PUT label — a real defect that slips through constantly because the status code is still 200.</p>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"What does idempotent mean, and which methods are idempotent?","a":"\n<p><strong>Idempotent</strong> — sending the same request N times leaves the server in the same state as sending it once. <strong>Safe</strong> — the request doesn't change state at all.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Method</th><th>Safe</th><th>Idempotent</th></tr>\n<tr><td>GET, HEAD, OPTIONS</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PUT</td><td>No</td><td>Yes — same replacement each time</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DELETE</td><td>No</td><td>Yes — gone stays gone (2nd call gives 404/204)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>POST</td><td>No</td><td>No — creates a new record each time</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PATCH</td><td>No</td><td>Not necessarily — depends on the operation</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>PATCH is idempotent if it sets a value (<code>status = \"APPROVED\"</code>) and not idempotent if it increments one (<code>balance += 100</code>).</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Why it matters</b>It's the whole reason retries are dangerous on POST. Fire the same payment POST twice on a network timeout and you've double-charged the customer — which is why real APIs use an idempotency key header. Ask about it in the interview; it shows payments experience.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"Explain HTTP status codes — the families and the ones you see daily.","a":"\n<table>\n<tr><th>Range</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Common codes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1xx</td><td>Informational</td><td>100 Continue — rarely seen in testing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2xx</td><td>Success</td><td>200 OK, 201 Created, 202 Accepted, 204 No Content</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3xx</td><td>Redirection</td><td>301 Moved Permanently, 302 Found, 304 Not Modified</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4xx</td><td>Client error — your request is wrong</td><td>400, 401, 403, 404, 405, 409, 415, 422, 429</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5xx</td><td>Server error — the request was fine, the server broke</td><td>500, 502, 503, 504</td></tr>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>400</strong> Bad Request — malformed JSON, missing required field.</li>\n<li><strong>405</strong> Method Not Allowed — right URL, wrong verb.</li>\n<li><strong>409</strong> Conflict — duplicate email, version mismatch.</li>\n<li><strong>415</strong> Unsupported Media Type — wrong Content-Type header.</li>\n<li><strong>422</strong> Unprocessable Entity — valid JSON, invalid business data (age = -5).</li>\n<li><strong>429</strong> Too Many Requests — rate limit hit.</li>\n<li><strong>502 / 503 / 504</strong> — bad gateway, service unavailable, gateway timeout: usually infrastructure, not the endpoint.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Defect triage</b>4xx means raise it with whoever wrote the client or the test. 5xx is always a server-side defect — even if you sent garbage, the API should return 400, not blow up.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"401 vs 403 vs 404 — and 400 vs 422?","a":"\n<p><strong>401 Unauthorized</strong> — authentication missing, expired or invalid. The server doesn't know who you are. Correct response includes a <code>WWW-Authenticate</code> header.</p>\n<p><strong>403 Forbidden</strong> — authentication succeeded, authorisation failed. The server knows exactly who you are and is refusing. Re-sending credentials won't help.</p>\n<p><strong>404 Not Found</strong> — the resource doesn't exist. Some APIs deliberately return 404 instead of 403 so attackers can't discover which record IDs exist.</p>\n<pre><code>No token          -&gt; 401\nExpired token     -&gt; 401\nValid token, but a read-only user calling DELETE -&gt; 403\nValid admin token, ID that doesn't exist         -&gt; 404</code></pre>\n<p><strong>400 vs 422:</strong> 400 is a syntax problem — the server couldn't parse the request. 422 is a semantic problem — it parsed fine, but the values fail business validation.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Interview trap</b>They'll describe a scenario and ask which code you'd expect. The tell is whether the server identified the caller: unknown caller = 401, known-but-refused = 403.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"What are the core principles of REST?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Client–server</strong> — UI and data storage evolve independently.</li>\n<li><strong>Stateless</strong> — every request carries everything needed; the server holds no session between calls. This is why the token goes on <em>every</em> request.</li>\n<li><strong>Uniform interface</strong> — resources as nouns in the URL, standard HTTP verbs for the actions.</li>\n<li><strong>Cacheable</strong> — responses declare whether they can be cached (ETag, Cache-Control).</li>\n<li><strong>Layered system</strong> — the client can't tell whether it's hitting the server directly or a gateway/load balancer.</li>\n<li><strong>Code on demand</strong> (optional, rarely used).</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>Good:  GET  /customers/101/orders\nBad:   GET  /getCustomerOrders?id=101      # verb in the URL\nBad:   POST /customers/101/delete          # verb in the URL again</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Testing angle</b>Statelessness is directly testable: fire two requests in any order, in parallel, from different clients — if the second one depends on the first having been made, the API isn't stateless and it won't survive load balancing.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"REST vs SOAP vs GraphQL?","a":"\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>REST</th><th>SOAP</th><th>GraphQL</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Format</td><td>Usually JSON</td><td>XML only</td><td>JSON</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Contract</td><td>OpenAPI/Swagger (optional)</td><td>WSDL (strict)</td><td>Schema (strict, typed)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Endpoints</td><td>Many, one per resource</td><td>One, action in the envelope</td><td>One, query defines the shape</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Testing focus</td><td>Verbs, status codes, JSON body</td><td>Envelope structure, XSD, faults</td><td>Query/mutation, partial errors</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>SOAP still appears in enterprise and financial systems for its built-in standards — WS-Security, transactions, guaranteed contract via WSDL. GraphQL solves over-fetching by letting the client ask for exactly the fields it needs.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>GraphQL gotcha</b>It returns <strong>200 OK even on failure</strong>, with the problem inside an <code>errors</code> array in the body. A test that only asserts the status code will pass on every broken query.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"What do you validate in an API test beyond the status code?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status code</strong> — and that it's the <em>right</em> one: 201 for a create, not a lazy 200.</li>\n<li><strong>Response body values</strong> — the data you sent came back, IDs generated, calculated fields correct.</li>\n<li><strong>Schema and data types</strong> — field present, correct type, required fields non-null, enums within range.</li>\n<li><strong>Headers</strong> — Content-Type, Location on a create, caching headers, security headers.</li>\n<li><strong>Response time</strong> — against the agreed SLA, not a vibe.</li>\n<li><strong>Persistence</strong> — GET it back, or check the DB. A 201 does not prove it was saved.</li>\n<li><strong>Error contract</strong> — failures return a structured, readable message with a code, not a stack trace.</li>\n<li><strong>No data leakage</strong> — password hashes, internal IDs and PII should not be in the response.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Say this</b>The single most common weak API suite is one that asserts 200 and nothing else. It goes green while the payload silently returns an empty array.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"Positive vs negative API testing — give me the scenarios you'd write.","a":"\n<p><strong>Positive:</strong> valid payload, valid auth, expected data returned, expected status code, record actually persisted.</p>\n<p><strong>Negative — the ones that find bugs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Missing required field</strong> — expect 400, not 500.</li>\n<li><strong>Wrong data type</strong> — string where a number is expected.</li>\n<li><strong>Malformed JSON</strong> — a trailing comma or unclosed brace.</li>\n<li><strong>Boundary values</strong> — empty string, 0, negative numbers, max length + 1, huge payload.</li>\n<li><strong>Auth cases</strong> — no token, expired token, malformed token, another user's token (that last one is the real security test).</li>\n<li><strong>Non-existent resource ID</strong> — expect 404, and no stack trace.</li>\n<li><strong>Wrong HTTP method</strong> on a valid URL — expect 405.</li>\n<li><strong>Wrong Content-Type</strong> — expect 415.</li>\n<li><strong>Duplicate create</strong> — expect 409, not a second record.</li>\n<li><strong>Special characters and injection strings</strong> in text fields.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Any 5xx during negative testing is a defect by definition. The API should reject bad input gracefully, and the error message should help the caller without exposing internals.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"What authentication methods have you tested, and what do you check for each?","a":"\n<table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>How it's sent</th></tr>\n<tr><td>API Key</td><td>Header (<code>x-api-key</code>) or query param — query params leak into logs, worth raising</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Basic Auth</td><td><code>Authorization: Basic base64(user:pass)</code> — encoded, not encrypted</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bearer / JWT</td><td><code>Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGci...</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>OAuth 2.0</td><td>Token fetched from an auth server, then sent as Bearer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Session / cookie</td><td>Cookie header — common where an API backs a web UI</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>What to test for every one of them:</strong> valid credentials pass; missing, expired, malformed and revoked credentials all return 401; a valid token for a <em>different</em> user cannot read or modify this user's data; a low-privilege token gets 403 on privileged endpoints; and the refresh flow issues a working token.</p>\n<pre><code>// A JWT is three base64 segments: header.payload.signature\n// The payload is readable by anyone — decode it and check\n// exp (expiry), roles and user id are what you expect.\n// Never treat \"it's a token\" as \"it's encrypted\".</code></pre>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"Which request and response headers matter when you're testing?","a":"\n<p><strong>Request headers:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>Content-Type</code> — the format you are <em>sending</em> (<code>application/json</code>).</li>\n<li><code>Accept</code> — the format you want <em>back</em>. Different header, constantly confused.</li>\n<li><code>Authorization</code> — credentials.</li>\n<li><code>Correlation-Id</code> / <code>X-Request-Id</code> — trace one call across microservices; invaluable for defect reports.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Response headers:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>Content-Type</code> — confirm it matches what the body actually is.</li>\n<li><code>Location</code> — the URL of the newly created resource on a 201.</li>\n<li><code>ETag</code> / <code>Cache-Control</code> — caching and conditional requests.</li>\n<li><code>X-RateLimit-Remaining</code> / <code>Retry-After</code> — throttling.</li>\n<li>Security: <code>Strict-Transport-Security</code>, <code>X-Content-Type-Options</code>, and <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> — a wildcard <code>*</code> on an authenticated API is worth flagging.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Quick win</b>Send a valid JSON body with <code>Content-Type: text/plain</code>. A well-built API returns 415. A surprising number return 500 — that's a bug you found in thirty seconds.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"Walk me through how you test an API manually in Postman.","a":"\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Read the contract first</strong> — Swagger/OpenAPI: endpoints, required fields, types, auth, expected codes. Import it straight into Postman to generate the requests.</li>\n<li><strong>Set up an environment</strong> — <code>{{baseUrl}}</code>, <code>{{token}}</code>, <code>{{userId}}</code> as variables so the same collection runs against QA, UAT and prod-like.</li>\n<li><strong>Auth once</strong> — a login request with a test script that saves the token into an environment variable, so every later request reuses it.</li>\n<li><strong>Build the CRUD chain</strong> — POST creates and saves the ID, GET verifies persistence, PUT/PATCH updates, GET confirms the change, DELETE removes, final GET returns 404.</li>\n<li><strong>Add assertions</strong> in the Tests tab — status code, schema, key values, response time.</li>\n<li><strong>Run negative cases</strong> — the list from the negative-testing question.</li>\n<li><strong>Run the collection</strong> with Collection Runner + a CSV/JSON data file for data-driven checks.</li>\n<li><strong>Hand it to CI</strong> — export and run with Newman, or port the coverage into Rest Assured.</li>\n</ol>\n<pre><code>// Postman test script — save the token for the next request\npm.test(\"Login succeeds\", () =&gt; pm.response.to.have.status(200));\npm.environment.set(\"token\", pm.response.json().token);\n\n// Typical assertions\npm.test(\"Status 201\", () =&gt; pm.response.to.have.status(201));\npm.test(\"Under 2s\",   () =&gt; pm.expect(pm.response.responseTime).to.be.below(2000));\npm.test(\"Has id\",     () =&gt; pm.expect(pm.response.json()).to.have.property('id'));</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Manual Postman work isn't throwaway. Structure the collection so it becomes the specification for the automated suite — chained, variable-driven, environment-agnostic.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"There's no documentation for the API. How do you test it?","a":"\n<p>It happens more often than anyone admits. In order:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Watch the UI</strong> — open DevTools Network tab, drive the feature, and copy the real calls (right-click → copy as cURL, import into Postman). That gives you real URLs, headers and payloads immediately.</li>\n<li><strong>Probe the endpoint</strong> — <code>OPTIONS</code> tells you which methods are allowed; a deliberately empty POST usually returns a validation error listing the required fields.</li>\n<li><strong>Read the code or the contract file</strong> — controllers, route definitions, or an unpublished swagger endpoint (many services expose one at /swagger or /v3/api-docs).</li>\n<li><strong>Check logs and the DB schema</strong> — column names and constraints tell you the field types and what's mandatory.</li>\n<li><strong>Ask the developer once, precisely</strong> — bring your inferred contract and ask them to correct it rather than asking them to write documentation.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Then write the contract down and get it reviewed. Turning an undocumented API into a documented one is the real deliverable — the tests are a by-product.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"How do you test asynchronous APIs, callbacks and webhooks?","a":"\n<p>Async endpoints return <strong>202 Accepted</strong> with a job or correlation id — the work isn't done yet, so asserting the final outcome on that response is the classic mistake.</p>\n<pre><code>POST /reports          -&gt; 202 Accepted  { \"jobId\": \"abc123\", \"status\": \"PENDING\" }\nGET  /reports/abc123   -&gt; 200 { \"status\": \"PROCESSING\" }\nGET  /reports/abc123   -&gt; 200 { \"status\": \"COMPLETE\", \"url\": \"...\" }</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Poll with a timeout</strong> — repeat the status GET at an interval until it's terminal or the deadline passes. Never a fixed sleep.</li>\n<li><strong>Test the failure path</strong> — a job that fails should end in a FAILED status with a reason, not hang in PENDING forever.</li>\n<li><strong>Webhooks</strong> — stand up a receiver (a test endpoint, or a service like webhook.site during manual testing), trigger the event, and assert the payload, signature header and delivery timing.</li>\n<li><strong>Retries and duplicates</strong> — most webhook providers deliver at-least-once, so verify the consumer is idempotent and handles the same event twice.</li>\n<li><strong>Queue-backed flows</strong> — verify the message actually landed on the queue or topic, not just that the API returned 202.</li>\n</ul>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"How would you test rate limiting, pagination and timeouts?","a":"\n<p><strong>Rate limiting:</strong> fire requests past the documented threshold and confirm a <code>429</code>, check <code>X-RateLimit-Remaining</code> decrements accurately, confirm <code>Retry-After</code> is present, and verify access is restored after the window resets. Also check the limit is per-user or per-key, not global — a shared limit means one client can starve everyone.</p>\n<p><strong>Pagination:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>GET /orders?page=1&amp;size=20\nGET /orders?offset=0&amp;limit=20</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Page size honoured; total count and total pages correct.</li>\n<li>No duplicate or missing records across page boundaries.</li>\n<li>Out-of-range page returns an empty list, not an error or the last page.</li>\n<li>Invalid values — <code>size=0</code>, <code>size=-1</code>, <code>size=999999</code> — handled sensibly.</li>\n<li>Sort order stable across pages, otherwise records shuffle between requests.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Timeouts:</strong> confirm the client timeout is shorter than the gateway timeout, that a slow downstream returns 504 rather than hanging, and that a timed-out create didn't actually create the record server-side — the classic duplicate-order bug.</p>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"What security checks do you run as part of normal API testing?","a":"\n<p>Not a penetration test — these are the checks a QA lead should own as routine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Broken object-level authorisation</strong> — log in as user A, call a resource belonging to user B by changing the id in the URL. Expect 403 or 404. This is the single most common serious API defect.</li>\n<li><strong>Privilege escalation</strong> — a standard user calling admin endpoints, or sending <code>\"role\": \"ADMIN\"</code> in a profile update payload and seeing whether the server accepts it.</li>\n<li><strong>Token handling</strong> — expired and revoked tokens rejected; logout actually invalidates; token not accepted after a password change.</li>\n<li><strong>Data exposure</strong> — no password hashes, internal ids, stack traces or full PII in responses or error messages.</li>\n<li><strong>Transport</strong> — HTTPS enforced, HTTP redirected or refused; no credentials in query strings (they end up in logs and browser history).</li>\n<li><strong>Injection strings</strong> in text fields — the response should be a clean validation error, never a database error message.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Frame these as regression tests, not a security audit. They're cheap, they run every build, and they catch the class of bug the UI can never reveal because the button was simply hidden.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"How do you validate that an API call actually persisted the data?","a":"\n<p>A 201 means the request was accepted, not that the record is correct or complete. Three levels of confirmation, in increasing strength:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Read-back</strong> — GET the resource by the id from the create response and compare every field you sent. This is the default and covers most cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Database check</strong> — query the table directly for fields the API doesn't return: audit columns, status flags, foreign keys, soft-delete markers. Essential when the API hides part of the record.</li>\n<li><strong>Downstream check</strong> — confirm the event reached the next system (queue, reporting table, partner feed) when the business flow spans services.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>-- Typical post-create DB assertion\nSELECT status, created_by, is_deleted\nFROM   bookings\nWHERE  booking_id = ?;</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Caution</b>Keep DB assertions to verification only, never setup. Tests that insert rows directly bypass business logic and pass while the real API is broken.</div>"},
{"c":"apitest","q":"Why test at the API layer at all when there's a UI suite?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Speed</strong> — roughly 10× faster than the equivalent UI test, so regression drops from hours to minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Stability</strong> — no locators, no rendering, no waits. Most flakiness lives in the browser layer.</li>\n<li><strong>Earlier feedback</strong> — the API is usually ready weeks before the screens, so testing can start before there's a UI to click.</li>\n<li><strong>Better coverage</strong> — you can hit error paths, boundary values and permission combinations the UI physically prevents you from sending.</li>\n<li><strong>Security-relevant</strong> — the UI hides a delete button from a read-only user; only an API test proves the server actually refuses the call.</li>\n<li><strong>Setup for UI tests</strong> — create the customer, policy or order via API in seconds instead of clicking through six screens.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Frame it as the middle of the test pyramid. UI tests should only cover what genuinely needs a browser — rendering, navigation, real user journeys. Everything else belongs one layer down, and moving it there is usually the single biggest regression-time win available.</div>"},
{"c":"api","q":"How do you validate an API end-to-end with Rest Assured?","a":"\n<p>Check status code, headers, body, schema and response time — not just 200 OK:</p>\n<pre><code>given()\n    .header(\"Authorization\", \"Bearer \" + token)\n    .contentType(ContentType.JSON)\n    .body(payload)\n.when()\n    .post(\"/users\")\n.then()\n    .statusCode(201)\n    .body(\"name\", equalTo(\"Karthik\"))\n    .body(\"id\", notNullValue())\n    .body(matchesJsonSchemaInClasspath(\"user-schema.json\"))\n    .time(lessThan(2000L));</code></pre>\n<p>Then chain: create → GET to confirm persistence → PUT → DELETE → GET returns 404. That's the real end-to-end contract.</p>"},
{"c":"api","q":"How do you handle authentication (OAuth2, tokens) in Rest Assured?","a":"\n<p>Fetch the token once in suite setup, cache it, inject it via a spec:</p>\n<pre><code>RequestSpecification authSpec = new RequestSpecBuilder()\n        .setBaseUri(config.get(\"baseUri\"))\n        .setContentType(ContentType.JSON)\n        .addHeader(\"Authorization\", \"Bearer \" + TokenManager.get())\n        .build();\n\ngiven().spec(authSpec).when().get(\"/orders\").then().statusCode(200);</code></pre>\n<p>Rest Assured also has <code>.auth().oauth2(token)</code>, <code>.basic()</code>, <code>.preemptive()</code>. Credentials come from environment variables or a vault — never from the repo. Handle expiry with a refresh check inside TokenManager.</p>"},
{"c":"api","q":"RequestSpecification vs ResponseSpecification — why bother?","a":"\n<p>They remove duplication and make suites consistent. Request spec centralises base URI, headers, auth, logging filters. Response spec centralises common assertions:</p>\n<pre><code>ResponseSpecification okSpec = new ResponseSpecBuilder()\n        .expectStatusCode(200)\n        .expectContentType(ContentType.JSON)\n        .expectResponseTime(lessThan(3000L))\n        .build();\n\ngiven().spec(authSpec).get(\"/users\").then().spec(okSpec);</code></pre>\n<p>Change the base URI or add a header in one place instead of 200 tests.</p>"},
{"c":"api","q":"How do you serialise and deserialise POJOs, and why prefer it over raw JSON strings?","a":"\n<pre><code>// Serialise: POJO -&gt; request body\nUser user = new User(\"Karthik\", \"QA Lead\");\ngiven().body(user)...          // Jackson/Gson handles conversion\n\n// Deserialise: response -&gt; POJO\nUser created = response.as(User.class);\nassertEquals(created.getRole(), \"QA Lead\");</code></pre>\n<p>POJOs give compile-time safety, IDE autocomplete, and refactor support — a renamed field breaks the build rather than failing mysteriously at runtime. Raw strings are fine only for quick negative payloads (malformed JSON tests).</p>"},
{"c":"api","q":"JsonPath assertions vs JSON schema validation — when do you use each?","a":"\n<p><strong>JsonPath</strong> checks values: <code>response.jsonPath().getString(\"data[0].id\")</code>, filters like <code>data.findAll { it.status == 'ACTIVE' }.size()</code>. Use for business assertions.</p>\n<p><strong>Schema validation</strong> checks structure and types — field presence, data types, required fields, enum values. Use it as a contract test so a backend change to a field type fails fast, in every test, without writing per-field assertions.</p>\n<p>In practice: schema for the contract, JsonPath for the specific business outcome.</p>"},
{"c":"api","q":"How do you test file upload/download, and chain dependent requests?","a":"\n<pre><code>// Multipart upload\ngiven().multiPart(\"file\", new File(\"src/test/resources/doc.pdf\"))\n       .when().post(\"/documents\")\n       .then().statusCode(201);\n\n// Chaining: capture then reuse\nString orderId = given().spec(authSpec).body(order)\n        .post(\"/orders\").jsonPath().getString(\"id\");\n\ngiven().spec(authSpec).pathParam(\"id\", orderId)\n        .get(\"/orders/{id}\").then().statusCode(200);</code></pre>\n<p>Download: assert content type and byte length via <code>response.asByteArray()</code>. Keep the chained ID in a thread-safe context object, not a static field.</p>"},
{"c":"api","q":"How do you integrate API automation with the rest of the suite?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Setup/teardown for UI tests</strong> — create the user, order or policy via API in seconds instead of clicking.</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-layer validation</strong> — do it in the UI, verify it landed correctly via API or DB.</li>\n<li><strong>Shift regression down</strong> — anything that doesn't need the browser becomes an API test.</li>\n<li><strong>Contract checks in CI</strong> — schema tests run on every backend build, well before UI regression.</li>\n</ul>"},
{"c":"api","q":"Path parameters vs query parameters?","a":"\n<p><strong>Path params</strong> identify a specific resource; <strong>query params</strong> filter, sort or paginate a collection.</p>\n<pre><code>// GET /booking/{id}\ngiven().spec(authSpec)\n       .pathParam(\"id\", bookingId)\n   .when().get(\"/booking/{id}\")\n   .then().statusCode(200);\n\n// GET /booking?firstname=Karthik&amp;checkin=2026-08-01\ngiven().spec(authSpec)\n       .queryParam(\"firstname\", \"Karthik\")\n       .queryParam(\"checkin\", \"2026-08-01\")\n   .when().get(\"/booking\")\n   .then().body(\"size()\", greaterThan(0));</code></pre>\n<p>Rule of thumb: if removing it would make the URL point at something else entirely, it's a path param.</p>"},
{"c":"api","q":"Basic auth in Rest Assured — preemptive vs challenged?","a":"\n<p><strong>Preemptive</strong> sends the Authorization header with the first request. <strong>Challenged</strong> waits for a 401 and then resends with credentials — an extra round trip, and it fails outright against servers that don't issue a challenge.</p>\n<pre><code>// Preemptive — what you almost always want\ngiven().auth().preemptive().basic(username, password)\n   .when().get(\"/accounts\")\n   .then().statusCode(200);\n\n// Challenged\ngiven().auth().basic(username, password) ...\n\n// Others\ngiven().auth().oauth2(token);\ngiven().auth().form(user, pass, new FormAuthConfig(\"/login\", \"user\", \"pass\"));</code></pre>"},
{"c":"api","q":"How do you build a request payload — String, Map/JSONObject, or POJO?","a":"\n<pre><code>// 1. Text block — quick, good for malformed/negative cases\nString body = \"\"\"\n    { \"firstname\": \"Karthik\", \"totalprice\": 500 }\n    \"\"\";\n\n// 2. Map or JSONObject — dynamic fields, no class needed\nJSONObject payload = new JSONObject();\npayload.put(\"firstname\", \"Karthik\");\npayload.put(\"totalprice\", 500);\n\n// 3. POJO — the default for maintained suites\nBooking booking = new Booking(\"Karthik\", 500, true);\ngiven().spec(authSpec).body(booking).post(\"/booking\");</code></pre>\n<p>POJOs win for anything long-lived: compile-time safety, IDE support, and a field rename breaks the build instead of failing at runtime. Serialisation (object → JSON) and deserialisation (<code>response.as(Booking.class)</code>) are handled by Jackson/Gson automatically.</p>"},
{"c":"api","q":"What can you pull out of the Response object?","a":"\n<pre><code>Response response = given().spec(authSpec).when().get(\"/booking/101\");\n\nint status        = response.getStatusCode();\nString contentType= response.getContentType();\nlong timeMs       = response.getTime();\nHeaders headers   = response.getHeaders();\nString server     = response.getHeader(\"Server\");\nString sessionId  = response.getCookie(\"SESSIONID\");\nString name       = response.jsonPath().getString(\"firstname\");\nList&lt;String&gt; ids  = response.jsonPath().getList(\"bookings.bookingid\");\nBooking booking   = response.as(Booking.class);\n\nresponse.prettyPrint();      // debugging only</code></pre>\n<p>Capturing the Response first (instead of chaining straight into <code>.then()</code>) is what lets you reuse values across a chained workflow.</p>"},
{"c":"testng","q":"What is the TestNG annotation execution order?","a":"\n<pre><code>@BeforeSuite → @BeforeTest → @BeforeClass → @BeforeMethod\n      → @Test →\n@AfterMethod → @AfterClass → @AfterTest → @AfterSuite</code></pre>\n<p>Typical mapping: <code>@BeforeSuite</code> start reports/load config · <code>@BeforeClass</code> launch browser · <code>@BeforeMethod</code> navigate/login · <code>@AfterMethod</code> screenshot on failure, reset state · <code>@AfterSuite</code> flush reports.</p>"},
{"c":"testng","q":"How does @DataProvider work?","a":"\n<pre><code>@DataProvider(name = \"loginData\", parallel = true)\npublic Object[][] getData() {\n    return new Object[][] {\n        {\"admin\", \"admin123\"},\n        {\"user1\", \"pass123\"}\n    };\n}\n\n@Test(dataProvider = \"loginData\")\npublic void loginTest(String user, String pass) {\n    loginPage.login(user, pass);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The test runs once per data set. Keep providers in a separate class (<code>dataProviderClass = TestData.class</code>) and feed them from JSON/Excel so data lives outside the code.</p>"},
{"c":"testng","q":"@Factory vs @DataProvider?","a":"\n<p><code>@DataProvider</code> runs the <strong>same method</strong> with different data. <code>@Factory</code> creates <strong>multiple instances of a test class</strong> with different constructor arguments — useful when the entire class should run against different configs (per browser, per product type).</p>\n<p>They combine: Factory produces the class instances, DataProvider feeds each method inside them.</p>"},
{"c":"testng","q":"How do you configure parallel execution in TestNG?","a":"\n<pre><code>&lt;suite name=\"Regression\" parallel=\"methods\" thread-count=\"4\"&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>Modes: <code>methods</code>, <code>classes</code>, <code>tests</code> (each <code>&lt;test&gt;</code> tag), <code>instances</code>. Pair with ThreadLocal drivers and independent data.</p>\n<p>Also useful: <code>@Test(threadPoolSize = 3, invocationCount = 9)</code> to hammer a single test across threads — handy for concurrency checks.</p>"},
{"c":"testng","q":"Explain dependsOnMethods and its risk.","a":"\n<pre><code>@Test\npublic void login() { }\n\n@Test(dependsOnMethods = \"login\")\npublic void createOrder() { }</code></pre>\n<p>If <code>login</code> fails, <code>createOrder</code> is <strong>skipped</strong>, not failed — which keeps the report honest. <code>dependsOnGroups</code> works at group level; <code>alwaysRun = true</code> forces execution regardless.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk:</strong> coupling. Dependent tests can't run in isolation or in parallel freely, and one failure cascades. Use only for genuine workflow chains; otherwise make each test set up its own state.</p>"},
{"c":"testng","q":"How do you implement retry for flaky tests?","a":"\n<pre><code>public class RetryAnalyzer implements IRetryAnalyzer {\n    private int count = 0;\n    private static final int MAX = 2;\n\n    @Override\n    public boolean retry(ITestResult result) {\n        return count++ &lt; MAX;\n    }\n}\n\n@Test(retryAnalyzer = RetryAnalyzer.class)\npublic void flakyTest() { }</code></pre>\n<p>Apply globally with <code>IAnnotationTransformer</code> so you don't annotate every test.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Retry is a band-aid. Log every retried test and review the list weekly — otherwise you're hiding real product race conditions.</div>"},
{"c":"testng","q":"What are TestNG listeners and which have you used?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><code>ITestListener</code> — onTestFailure/Success/Skipped: screenshots, report logging.</li>\n<li><code>IRetryAnalyzer</code> — retries.</li>\n<li><code>IAnnotationTransformer</code> — modify annotations at runtime (global retry, dynamic timeouts).</li>\n<li><code>ISuiteListener</code> — suite-level setup/teardown.</li>\n<li><code>IReporter</code> — custom report generation.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>public class TestListener implements ITestListener {\n    @Override public void onTestFailure(ITestResult result) {\n        ScreenshotUtil.capture(result.getName());\n        ExtentManager.get().fail(result.getThrowable());\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Register via <code>@Listeners</code> or the <code>&lt;listeners&gt;</code> block in testng.xml.</p>"},
{"c":"testng","q":"How do groups work, and how do you build tiered suites?","a":"\n<pre><code>@Test(groups = {\"smoke\", \"regression\"})\npublic void loginTest() { }</code></pre>\n<pre><code>&lt;groups&gt;\n  &lt;run&gt;\n    &lt;include name=\"smoke\"/&gt;\n    &lt;exclude name=\"wip\"/&gt;\n  &lt;/run&gt;\n&lt;/groups&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>One codebase serves smoke (per commit), sanity (per deploy) and regression (nightly). <code>@BeforeGroups</code>/<code>@AfterGroups</code> handle group-specific setup.</p>"},
{"c":"testng","q":"Soft assert vs hard assert?","a":"\n<p><strong>Hard</strong> (<code>Assert.assertEquals</code>) stops the test at the first failure. <strong>Soft</strong> collects failures and reports them together at <code>assertAll()</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>SoftAssert soft = new SoftAssert();\nsoft.assertEquals(page.getTitle(), \"Dashboard\");\nsoft.assertTrue(page.isLogoDisplayed());\nsoft.assertAll();   // omit this and the test passes silently</code></pre>\n<p>Soft for validating many fields on one screen; hard for preconditions where continuing is pointless. The forgotten <code>assertAll()</code> is a classic interview trap.</p>"},
{"c":"testng","q":"How do you pass parameters from testng.xml?","a":"\n<pre><code>&lt;parameter name=\"browser\" value=\"chrome\"/&gt;</code></pre>\n<pre><code>@BeforeMethod\n@Parameters(\"browser\")\npublic void setup(@Optional(\"chrome\") String browser) { }</code></pre>\n<p><code>@Optional</code> supplies a default when the parameter is absent. Rule of thumb: xml parameters for environment/browser config, DataProvider for test data sets.</p>"},
{"c":"testng","q":"priority vs dependsOnMethods vs invocationCount — clear them up.","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><code>priority</code> — ordering hint only (lower runs first). No failure relationship. Default is 0, so unprioritised tests run before priority 1.</li>\n<li><code>dependsOnMethods</code> — real dependency: failure upstream skips downstream.</li>\n<li><code>invocationCount</code> — runs the same test N times; combine with <code>threadPoolSize</code> for concurrency, or <code>successPercentage</code> to tolerate partial failures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common gotcha: priority does not guarantee order across parallel threads.</p>"},
{"c":"testng","q":"Walk me through your testng.xml.","a":"\n<pre><code>&lt;suite name=\"Regression\" parallel=\"methods\" thread-count=\"4\"&gt;\n\n    &lt;parameter name=\"env\" value=\"staging\"/&gt;\n    &lt;parameter name=\"browser\" value=\"chrome\"/&gt;\n\n    &lt;listeners&gt;\n        &lt;listener class-name=\"com.qa.listeners.TestListener\"/&gt;\n        &lt;listener class-name=\"com.qa.listeners.RetryTransformer\"/&gt;\n    &lt;/listeners&gt;\n\n    &lt;test name=\"Smoke\"&gt;\n        &lt;groups&gt;\n            &lt;run&gt;\n                &lt;include name=\"smoke\"/&gt;\n                &lt;exclude name=\"wip\"/&gt;\n            &lt;/run&gt;\n        &lt;/groups&gt;\n        &lt;classes&gt;\n            &lt;class name=\"com.qa.tests.LoginTest\"/&gt;\n            &lt;class name=\"com.qa.tests.PaymentTest\"&gt;\n                &lt;methods&gt;&lt;exclude name=\"legacyFlow\"/&gt;&lt;/methods&gt;\n            &lt;/class&gt;\n        &lt;/classes&gt;\n    &lt;/test&gt;\n&lt;/suite&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>Keep several suite files — smoke.xml, regression.xml, api.xml — and point Maven at whichever the pipeline needs.</p>"},
{"c":"testng","q":"@BeforeSuite vs @BeforeTest vs @BeforeClass — what actually differs?","a":"\n<p>They map to the xml structure, not to intuition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>@BeforeSuite</strong> — once for the entire <code>&lt;suite&gt;</code>. Load config, start the report, spin up Grid.</li>\n<li><strong>@BeforeTest</strong> — once per <code>&lt;test&gt;</code> tag. Useful when each <code>&lt;test&gt;</code> is a browser or an environment.</li>\n<li><strong>@BeforeClass</strong> — once per test class. Launch the browser for that class.</li>\n<li><strong>@BeforeMethod</strong> — before every <code>@Test</code>. Navigate, log in, reset state.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Interviewers ask this because most people assume \"@BeforeTest runs before each test\" — it doesn't; that's @BeforeMethod.</p>"},
{"c":"maven","q":"Explain the Maven build lifecycle phases you care about.","a":"\n<pre><code>validate → compile → test → package → verify → install → deploy</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>mvn test</code> — runs unit-style tests via <strong>Surefire</strong>.</li>\n<li><code>mvn verify</code> — runs integration tests via <strong>Failsafe</strong> (post-package), then checks.</li>\n<li><code>mvn clean install</code> — full build into the local repo.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Running a later phase runs everything before it — that's why <code>install</code> triggers your tests.</p>"},
{"c":"maven","q":"What are the three Maven lifecycles?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>clean</strong> — <code>pre-clean, clean, post-clean</code>. Deletes <code>target/</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>default (build)</strong> — validate → compile → test → package → verify → install → deploy.</li>\n<li><strong>site</strong> — <code>site</code>, <code>site-deploy</code>: generates project documentation and reports.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>mvn clean                 # wipe target\nmvn clean test            # wipe, compile, run tests\nmvn clean install         # ... plus package and install to the local .m2 repo\nmvn clean verify -Pregression</code></pre>\n<p>Running a phase runs every phase before it in that lifecycle — which is why <code>install</code> executes your tests.</p>"},
{"c":"maven","q":"Difference between a Maven phase, a goal and a plugin?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Plugin</strong> — the thing that does the work (surefire, compiler, failsafe, dependency).</li>\n<li><strong>Goal</strong> — a single task inside a plugin, written <code>plugin:goal</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase</strong> — a stage in the lifecycle that goals bind to.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>mvn test                  # a PHASE — triggers surefire:test, which is bound to it\nmvn dependency:tree       # a GOAL — run directly, no lifecycle involved\nmvn surefire:test         # the same goal, invoked explicitly\nmvn help:effective-pom    # goal — shows the pom after inheritance is applied</code></pre>"},
{"c":"maven","q":"Surefire vs Failsafe — which do you use for automation suites?","a":"\n<table>\n<tr><th>Surefire</th><th>Failsafe</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Runs in <code>test</code> phase</td><td>Runs in <code>integration-test</code> / <code>verify</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fails the build immediately</td><td>Defers failure to <code>verify</code> so teardown still runs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Default pattern <code>*Test.java</code></td><td>Default pattern <code>*IT.java</code></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>UI/API suites belong in Failsafe when they need the app packaged/deployed first; most Selenium+TestNG teams still drive Surefire with a testng.xml pointer:</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;plugin&gt;\n  &lt;artifactId&gt;maven-surefire-plugin&lt;/artifactId&gt;\n  &lt;configuration&gt;\n    &lt;suiteXmlFiles&gt;&lt;suiteXmlFile&gt;testng.xml&lt;/suiteXmlFile&gt;&lt;/suiteXmlFiles&gt;\n  &lt;/configuration&gt;\n&lt;/plugin&gt;</code></pre>"},
{"c":"maven","q":"How do you run different suites/environments from the command line?","a":"\n<pre><code>mvn clean test -DsuiteXmlFile=smoke.xml -Denv=qa -Dbrowser=chrome</code></pre>\n<p>Read them in code with <code>System.getProperty(\"env\", \"qa\")</code>, falling back to a properties file. Or use <strong>profiles</strong>:</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;profiles&gt;\n  &lt;profile&gt;\n    &lt;id&gt;regression&lt;/id&gt;\n    &lt;properties&gt;&lt;suiteXmlFile&gt;regression.xml&lt;/suiteXmlFile&gt;&lt;/properties&gt;\n  &lt;/profile&gt;\n&lt;/profiles&gt;</code></pre>\n<pre><code>mvn clean verify -Pregression</code></pre>\n<p>This is what CI jobs call — same command locally and on the agent.</p>"},
{"c":"maven","q":"How do you run a specific class, suite or group from the command line?","a":"\n<pre><code># whole suite file\nmvn clean test -DsuiteXmlFile=testng.xml\n\n# one class, or one method\nmvn test -Dtest=LoginTest\nmvn test -Dtest=LoginTest#invalidPasswordTest\n\n# TestNG groups\nmvn test -Dgroups=smoke\n\n# browser + environment together\nmvn clean test -Dbrowser=chrome -Denv=staging -DsuiteXmlFile=regression.xml\n\n# keep going after a module fails, and skip tests on a packaging build\nmvn clean install -DskipTests</code></pre>\n<p>Whatever the pipeline runs, make sure it's the exact same command a developer can run locally — that's what stops \"works on my machine\".</p>"},
{"c":"maven","q":"How do you read -D parameters inside Java?","a":"\n<pre><code>// mvn test -Denv=staging -Dbrowser=chrome\nString env     = System.getProperty(\"env\", \"qa\");         // second arg = default\nString browser = System.getProperty(\"browser\", \"chrome\");</code></pre>\n<p>Wrap it so the whole framework reads config the same way, with a sensible precedence chain — command line beats environment variable beats properties file:</p>\n<pre><code>public static String get(String key) {\n    String value = System.getProperty(key);                 // -D wins\n    if (value == null) value = System.getenv(key.toUpperCase());\n    if (value == null) value = properties.getProperty(key); // config.properties\n    return value;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Also available: <code>System.getenv()</code> for CI secrets, and <code>System.setProperty()</code> if you need to set one at runtime.</p>"},
{"c":"maven","q":"Explain Maven dependency scopes and how you resolve version conflicts.","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><code>compile</code> (default) — available everywhere.</li>\n<li><code>test</code> — TestNG, Rest Assured, AssertJ; not shipped.</li>\n<li><code>provided</code> — supplied at runtime by the container.</li>\n<li><code>runtime</code> — needed to run, not to compile (JDBC drivers).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For conflicts, Maven uses <strong>nearest-wins</strong>. Diagnose with <code>mvn dependency:tree</code>, then either <code>&lt;exclusions&gt;</code> the transitive offender or pin the version in <code>&lt;dependencyManagement&gt;</code>. Classic case: Rest Assured and Selenium both pulling different Guava/Jackson versions.</p>"},
{"c":"devops","q":"How do you integrate the suite into CI/CD?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trigger tiers</strong> — smoke on every PR/commit, sanity on deploy to QA, full regression nightly.</li>\n<li><strong>Quality gates</strong> — fail the build on smoke failure; regression failures raise a report and notify, not necessarily block.</li>\n<li><strong>Containerise</strong> — run tests in Docker with Selenium Grid/Selenoid so agents are identical.</li>\n<li><strong>Artifacts</strong> — publish Extent/Allure HTML, screenshots, videos, and JUnit XML for the pipeline's native test view.</li>\n<li><strong>Notify</strong> — Teams/Slack summary with pass rate and a link to the report.</li>\n<li><strong>Secrets</strong> — credentials from the pipeline's secret store, never in the pom or repo.</li>\n</ul>"},
{"c":"devops","q":"Git: difference between fetch and pull?","a":"\n<p><code>git fetch</code> downloads remote commits into your local tracking branches but <strong>doesn't touch your working directory</strong>. <code>git pull</code> is <code>fetch</code> + <code>merge</code> (or rebase) — it changes your files immediately.</p>\n<pre><code>git fetch origin              # see what's new, safely\ngit diff main origin/main     # review before integrating\ngit merge origin/main         # now apply it\n\ngit pull                      # both steps at once\ngit pull --rebase             # replay your commits on top — cleaner history</code></pre>\n<p>Fetch first is the safer habit when you have uncommitted work in progress.</p>"},
{"c":"devops","q":"Git: how do you push a local project to a remote repository?","a":"\n<pre><code>git init\ngit add .\ngit commit -m \"Initial automation framework\"\ngit branch -M main\ngit remote add origin https://github.com/org/qa-automation.git\ngit push -u origin main        # -u sets upstream, so later just 'git push'</code></pre>\n<p>Everyday flow after that:</p>\n<pre><code>git checkout -b feature/payment-tests\ngit add . && git commit -m \"Add payment regression tests\"\ngit push -u origin feature/payment-tests    # then raise a PR</code></pre>"},
{"c":"devops","q":"How do you manage branching for an automation repo?","a":"\n<ul>\n<li><strong>main protected</strong> — no direct pushes; every change goes through a PR with at least one reviewer.</li>\n<li><strong>Feature branches</strong> per story or defect, short-lived, named consistently (<code>feature/</code>, <code>fix/</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Smoke suite runs on every PR</strong> as a merge gate — automation code gets the same quality bar as product code.</li>\n<li><strong>Tag per release</strong> so you can reproduce exactly what ran against a given build.</li>\n<li><strong>.gitignore</strong> for <code>target/</code>, <code>test-output/</code>, reports, screenshots, and anything with credentials.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Secrets in the repo history is the failure that costs you the most — enforce a scanner in the PR pipeline.</div>"},
{"c":"devops","q":"How do you debug a failing automation test?","a":"\n<p>In the IDE:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Breakpoints</strong> — including conditional ones (<code>i == 47</code>) and exception breakpoints that trigger the moment NoSuchElementException is thrown.</li>\n<li><strong>Step over / into / out</strong> to follow the call into page objects.</li>\n<li><strong>Variable watches and Evaluate Expression</strong> — run <code>driver.findElements(locator).size()</code> live while paused on the failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Call stack</strong> to find which layer actually threw.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In CI, where you can't attach a debugger, the evidence has to be captured up front: screenshot and page source on failure, video/trace, request/response logs for API calls, and structured logging.</p>\n<pre><code>// Log4j2 / SLF4J levels, coarsest to finest\nERROR  → test or framework failure\nWARN   → retry happened, fallback used\nINFO   → test started, navigated, key business step\nDEBUG  → locator resolved, wait satisfied\nTRACE  → raw request/response payloads</code></pre>\n<p>Run INFO in CI, DEBUG locally. If a CI-only failure can't be diagnosed from the artifacts, that's a gap in the framework, not bad luck.</p>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Find the first non-repeating character in a string.","a":"<p>There are two optimal approaches in Java depending on whether you want an idiomatic Java Collections solution or a memory-optimized primitive array solution.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Approach 1: Using <code>LinkedHashMap</code> (Clean &amp; Preserves Insertion Order)</strong></p>\n<pre><code>import java.util.*;\nimport java.util.stream.Collectors;\n\npublic class FirstNonRepeating {\n    public static Character findFirstNonRepeating(String str) {\n        if (str == null || str.isEmpty()) return null;\n\n        // LinkedHashMap keeps the exact order characters were seen\n        Map&lt;Character, Integer&gt; counts = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;();\n\n        // Pass 1: Build frequency map\n        for (char ch : str.toCharArray()) {\n            counts.put(ch, counts.getOrDefault(ch, 0) + 1);\n        }\n\n        // Pass 2: Find the first entry with frequency 1\n        for (Map.Entry&lt;Character, Integer&gt; entry : counts.entrySet()) {\n            if (entry.getValue() == 1) {\n                return entry.getKey();\n            }\n        }\n        return null;\n    }\n\n    // Java 8+ Streams alternative:\n    public static Character findWithStream(String str) {\n        if (str == null || str.isEmpty()) return null;\n\n        Map&lt;Character, Long&gt; counts = str.chars()\n            .mapToObj(c -&gt; (char) c)\n            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(c -&gt; c, LinkedHashMap::new, Collectors.counting()));\n\n        return counts.entrySet().stream()\n            .filter(e -&gt; e.getValue() == 1L)\n            .map(Map.Entry::getKey)\n            .findFirst()\n            .orElse(null);\n    }\n}\n\n// Test Run:\nSystem.out.println(FirstNonRepeating.findFirstNonRepeating(\"aabbcde\")); // c\nSystem.out.println(FirstNonRepeating.findFirstNonRepeating(\"aabb\"));    // null</code></pre>\n\n<p><strong>Approach 2: Using an <code>int[256]</code> ASCII Array (Fastest &amp; Zero Heap Allocation)</strong></p>\n<pre><code>public static Character findOptimal(String str) {\n    if (str == null || str.isEmpty()) return null;\n\n    int[] freq = new int[256]; // covers all ASCII characters\n\n    // Count frequencies\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; str.length(); i++) {\n        freq[str.charAt(i)]++;\n    }\n\n    // Traverse string again to preserve first occurrence order\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; str.length(); i++) {\n        if (freq[str.charAt(i)] == 1) {\n            return str.charAt(i);\n        }\n    }\n    return null;\n}</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Complexity Analysis</b>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Time Complexity:</strong> O(n) — two linear passes over the string of length n.</li>\n<li><strong>Space Complexity:</strong> O(1) — fixed array size (256) or maximum 26 lowercase English letters.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Reverse a string without using built-in methods.","a":"<p>Interviewers specifically prohibit <code>new StringBuilder(s).reverse()</code>. You should demonstrate the <strong>two-pointer in-place swap</strong> on a <code>char[]</code> or the backward loop approach.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Approach 1: Two-Pointer In-Place Swap (Optimal O(n) Time, O(n) Space for char array)</strong></p>\n<pre><code>public class StringReversal {\n    public static String reverseTwoPointers(String str) {\n        if (str == null || str.length() &lt;= 1) return str;\n\n        char[] chars = str.toCharArray();\n        int left = 0;\n        int right = chars.length - 1;\n\n        while (left &lt; right) {\n            // Swap characters at left and right pointers\n            char temp = chars[left];\n            chars[left] = chars[right];\n            chars[right] = temp;\n\n            left++;\n            right--;\n        }\n\n        return new String(chars);\n    }\n}\n\n// Test Run:\nSystem.out.println(StringReversal.reverseTwoPointers(\"automation\")); // \"noitamotua\"\nSystem.out.println(StringReversal.reverseTwoPointers(\"Selenium\"));   // \"muineleS\"</code></pre>\n\n<p><strong>Approach 2: Backward Loop with <code>char[]</code> (Without any wrapper methods)</strong></p>\n<pre><code>public static String reverseBackward(String str) {\n    if (str == null) return null;\n\n    char[] orig = str.toCharArray();\n    char[] reversed = new char[orig.length];\n\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; orig.length; i++) {\n        reversed[i] = orig[orig.length - 1 - i];\n    }\n\n    return new String(reversed);\n}</code></pre>\n\n<p><strong>Approach 3: Recursive Reversal (Common Senior Interview Follow-up)</strong></p>\n<pre><code>public static String reverseRecursive(String str) {\n    if (str == null || str.length() &lt;= 1) return str;\n    // Recursive case: take last character + reverse of remaining substring\n    return reverseRecursive(str.substring(1)) + str.charAt(0);\n}</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Senior Follow-Up: Reverse Words in a Sentence</b>\nIf asked to reverse <em>\"Selenium Java Automation\"</em> to <em>\"Automation Java Selenium\"</em>:\nSplit by space <code>str.split(\"\\\\s+\")</code>, iterate the array backwards, and join using <code>String.join(\" \", words)</code> — runs in O(n) time.\n</div>"},
{"c":"manual","q":"Write test cases for a Login page.","a":"<p>Cover positive, negative, boundary, security and UI/UX scenarios.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>#</th><th>Scenario</th><th>Expected Result</th></tr>\n<tr><td>TC01</td><td>Valid username + valid password</td><td>Redirects to Dashboard, welcome message shown</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC02</td><td>Valid username + wrong password</td><td>Error: 'Invalid credentials', stays on login page</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC03</td><td>Wrong username + valid password</td><td>Error: 'Invalid credentials'</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC04</td><td>Both fields empty, click Login</td><td>Inline validation: 'Username is required'</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC05</td><td>Username filled, Password empty</td><td>Inline validation: 'Password is required'</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC06</td><td>Password field masked (•••)</td><td>Characters hidden by default</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC07</td><td>Show password toggle clicked</td><td>Password text visible</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC08</td><td>3 consecutive wrong password attempts</td><td>Account locked / CAPTCHA shown</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC09</td><td>SQL injection in username field</td><td>Treated as invalid credential, no DB error exposed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC10</td><td>Forgot Password link clicked</td><td>Navigates to password reset flow</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC11</td><td>Press Enter key after filling credentials</td><td>Form submits</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC12</td><td>Back button after login</td><td>Should NOT navigate back to login (session active)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC13</td><td>Login with expired credentials</td><td>Appropriate message asking user to reset password</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC14</td><td>Page loads correctly on mobile viewport</td><td>Responsive layout, form usable</td></tr>\n</table>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Group cases by risk area: auth logic, session management, security, UX. That makes it easier to prioritise for automation.</div>"},
{"c":"manual","q":"Write test cases for a Payment Gateway.","a":"<table>\n<tr><th>#</th><th>Scenario</th><th>Expected Result</th></tr>\n<tr><td>TC01</td><td>Valid card, sufficient balance</td><td>Payment Success, order confirmed, receipt emailed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC02</td><td>Valid card, insufficient balance</td><td>Payment Failed – 'Insufficient funds'</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC03</td><td>Expired card</td><td>Error: 'Card has expired'</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC04</td><td>Invalid card number (Luhn check fail)</td><td>Inline error: 'Invalid card number'</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC05</td><td>Wrong CVV</td><td>Payment declined</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC06</td><td>Card blocked by issuer</td><td>Payment declined with reason</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC07</td><td>International card, currency mismatch</td><td>DCC prompt or rejection with message</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC08</td><td>Payment amount = 0</td><td>Validation error, cannot proceed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC09</td><td>Negative payment amount</td><td>Validation error</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC10</td><td>Duplicate click of 'Pay Now' button</td><td>Only one transaction created (idempotency)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC11</td><td>Network timeout during payment</td><td>Pending state shown; no double charge</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC12</td><td>Browser back after payment confirmation</td><td>No duplicate charge on page reload</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC13</td><td>3D Secure / OTP flow</td><td>OTP screen shown, correct OTP completes payment</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC14</td><td>Refund for completed transaction</td><td>Refund initiated, amount credited within SLA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TC15</td><td>Card saved for future use</td><td>Card masked and tokenised in DB, not plain text</td></tr>\n</table>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Always test the network timeout / double-click scenario — it's the source of real-world double-charge incidents and rarely covered by developers.</div>"},
{"c":"manual","q":"Difference between Severity and Priority.","a":"<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Severity</th><th>Priority</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Definition</td><td>Impact on the system/user functionality</td><td>Urgency of fixing the defect</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Set by</td><td>QA / Tester</td><td>Product Owner / Business</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Based on</td><td>Technical impact</td><td>Business value &amp; visibility</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Classic mismatch examples:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High Severity, Low Priority</strong> — Crash on an admin-only feature used by 2 people monthly. Crash is severe technically, but business priority is low.</li>\n<li><strong>Low Severity, High Priority</strong> — Company logo is missing/broken on the homepage. Cosmetically minor, but business demands an immediate fix due to brand impact.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>// Defect report example\nSeverity: Critical  → payment API throws 500 on valid card\nPriority: P1        → fix before today's release cut-off\n\nSeverity: Minor     → copyright year shows 2023 instead of 2026\nPriority: P2        → fix in next sprint\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>One-liner</b>Severity is about HOW BAD it is technically. Priority is about HOW SOON it needs fixing. They are independent axes.</div>"},
{"c":"manual","q":"Difference between Smoke, Sanity and Regression Testing.","a":"<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Smoke Testing</th><th>Sanity Testing</th><th>Regression Testing</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Verify build is stable enough to test</td><td>Verify a specific fix/feature works</td><td>Ensure existing features still work</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Scope</td><td>Broad, shallow</td><td>Narrow, deep</td><td>Wide, thorough</td></tr>\n<tr><td>When</td><td>After every new build</td><td>After a bug fix / hotfix</td><td>After any code change</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Done by</td><td>QA (automated)</td><td>QA or Dev</td><td>QA (automated)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Duration</td><td>&lt;15 min</td><td>&lt;30 min</td><td>Hours (parallel)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Real-world analogy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Smoke</strong> = Turn on the car ignition. Does it start? If not, no point testing brakes.</li>\n<li><strong>Sanity</strong> = After mechanic fixed the brakes, just test the brakes specifically.</li>\n<li><strong>Regression</strong> = After the brake fix, test the entire car to confirm nothing else broke.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>In a CI pipeline</b>Smoke runs on every commit (gates the build), sanity runs post-hotfix, regression runs nightly or pre-release.</div>"},
{"c":"manual","q":"Explain the Bug Life Cycle.","a":"<p>The Bug Life Cycle (Defect Life Cycle) is the series of states a defect passes through from discovery to closure.</p>\n<pre><code>New → Assigned → Open → Fixed → Retest → Closed\n                   ↓                ↓\n                Deferred         Reopened → (back to Open)\n                   ↓\n                Rejected / Not a Bug / Duplicate</code></pre>\n<table>\n<tr><th>State</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Owner</th></tr>\n<tr><td>New</td><td>Defect logged for the first time</td><td>QA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Assigned</td><td>Bug assigned to a developer</td><td>Lead/PM</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Open</td><td>Developer is actively working on it</td><td>Dev</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fixed</td><td>Developer claims fix is done</td><td>Dev</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Retest</td><td>QA verifies the fix</td><td>QA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Closed</td><td>Fix verified, defect resolved</td><td>QA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reopened</td><td>Fix didn't work or regression found</td><td>QA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rejected</td><td>Not a bug, working as designed</td><td>Dev/PM</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deferred</td><td>Valid bug, fixed in a future release</td><td>PM</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Duplicate</td><td>Already reported bug</td><td>QA/Lead</td></tr>\n</table>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Track the Reopen Rate — if more than 10-15% of bugs get reopened, it signals poor fix quality or insufficient dev testing before handoff.</div>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"Write a Playwright script to login into an application.","a":"<p>Use the Page Object Model pattern with Playwright's built-in locators and auto-waiting.</p>\n<pre><code>// tests/login.spec.ts\nimport { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';\n\ntest('User can login successfully', async ({ page }) => {\n  await page.goto('https://example.com/login');\n\n  // Fill credentials\n  await page.getByLabel('Username').fill('admin@example.com');\n  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill('SecurePass123');\n\n  // Submit\n  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Login' }).click();\n\n  // Assert redirect to dashboard\n  await expect(page).toHaveURL(/.*dashboard/);\n  await expect(page.getByText('Welcome, Admin')).toBeVisible();\n});\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>// pages/LoginPage.ts – Page Object\nimport { Page, Locator } from '@playwright/test';\n\nexport class LoginPage {\n  private readonly usernameInput: Locator;\n  private readonly passwordInput: Locator;\n  private readonly loginButton: Locator;\n\n  constructor(private page: Page) {\n    this.usernameInput = page.getByLabel('Username');\n    this.passwordInput = page.getByLabel('Password');\n    this.loginButton   = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Login' });\n  }\n\n  async goto() { await this.page.goto('/login'); }\n\n  async login(username: string, password: string) {\n    await this.usernameInput.fill(username);\n    await this.passwordInput.fill(password);\n    await this.loginButton.click();\n  }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>// In test using POM\nconst loginPage = new LoginPage(page);\nawait loginPage.goto();\nawait loginPage.login('admin@example.com', 'SecurePass123');\nawait expect(page).toHaveURL(/dashboard/);\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Pro tip</b>Use storageState to save login session and skip the UI login for every test: <code>npx playwright test --global-setup globalSetup.ts</code></div>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"How do you handle dynamic locators in Playwright?","a":"<p>Dynamic elements change their IDs or attributes on every page load. Playwright provides multiple strategies to target them reliably.</p>\n<pre><code>// Strategy 1: getByRole — most resilient, based on ARIA\nawait page.getByRole('button', { name: /submit/i }).click();\n\n// Strategy 2: getByText — targets by visible text\nawait page.getByText('Proceed to Checkout').click();\n\n// Strategy 3: data-testid (request devs to add stable test IDs)\nawait page.getByTestId('order-confirm-btn').click();\n\n// Strategy 4: CSS attribute selectors for partial matches\nawait page.locator('[class*=\"active-tab\"]').click();\nawait page.locator('[data-order-id]').first().click();\n\n// Strategy 5: Filter + chaining for table rows\nawait page\n  .getByRole('row')\n  .filter({ hasText: 'Invoice #1234' })\n  .getByRole('button', { name: 'Download' })\n  .click();\n\n// Strategy 6: Regex for partial text or URL\nawait page.locator('button', { hasText: /confirm/i }).click();\n\n// Strategy 7: nth locator when there are multiple matches\nawait page.locator('.product-card').nth(2).click();\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Best practice</b>Work with your dev team to add <code>data-testid</code> attributes to key interactive elements. They're stable, semantic, and don't change with CSS refactors.</div>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"Difference between locator(), getByRole() and getByText().","a":"<table>\n<tr><th>Method</th><th>What it targets</th><th>When to use</th></tr>\n<tr><td><code>locator()</code></td><td>CSS selector, XPath, or other strategies</td><td>Fallback for complex selectors; shadow DOM; attribute-based targeting</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>getByRole()</code></td><td>ARIA role (button, link, heading, checkbox, etc.)</td><td>Primary choice – most aligned with how users &amp; screen-readers interact</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>getByText()</code></td><td>Exact or partial visible text content</td><td>Links, labels, headings with stable copy; use regex for partial</td></tr>\n</table>\n<pre><code>// locator() – flexible but verbose\nawait page.locator('input[data-testid=\"username\"]').fill('admin');\nawait page.locator('//button[@type=\"submit\"]').click(); // XPath\n\n// getByRole() – preferred for interactive elements\nawait page.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Email' }).fill('qa@test.com');\nawait page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Submit' }).click();\nawait page.getByRole('checkbox', { name: 'Remember me' }).check();\n\n// getByText() – great for assertions and navigation links\nawait page.getByText('Order Confirmed').waitFor();\nawait page.getByText(/sign in/i).click(); // case-insensitive regex\n\n// Other semantic locators\nawait page.getByLabel('Password').fill('pass123'); // form labels\nawait page.getByPlaceholder('Search products').fill('laptop');\nawait page.getByTestId('cart-total').textContent();\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Priority order</b>getByRole → getByLabel → getByPlaceholder → getByText → getByTestId → locator(CSS) → locator(XPath). Follow this to write the most resilient tests.</div>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"How do you upload a file using Playwright?","a":"<p>Playwright provides <code>setInputFiles()</code> for file input elements — no third-party libraries needed.</p>\n<pre><code>// Single file upload\nawait page.locator('input[type=\"file\"]').setInputFiles('tests/fixtures/invoice.pdf');\n\n// Multiple files\nawait page.locator('input[type=\"file\"]').setInputFiles([\n  'tests/fixtures/doc1.pdf',\n  'tests/fixtures/doc2.xlsx'\n]);\n\n// Clear previously selected files\nawait page.locator('input[type=\"file\"]').setInputFiles([]);\n\n// File upload with drag and drop trigger (some implementations)\nawait page.locator('input[type=\"file\"]').setInputFiles('report.csv');\nawait page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Upload' }).click();\n\n// Assert upload success\nawait expect(page.getByText('invoice.pdf')).toBeVisible();\nawait expect(page.getByText('Upload successful')).toBeVisible();\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>// When file input is hidden (disguised as a button)\nconst [fileChooser] = await Promise.all([\n  page.waitForEvent('filechooser'),\n  page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Attach File' }).click()\n]);\nawait fileChooser.setFiles('tests/fixtures/invoice.pdf');\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Common pitfall</b>File input elements are often hidden (<code>display:none</code>). Don't try to click the styled button through normal Playwright actions — use <code>waitForEvent('filechooser')</code> instead.</div>"},
{"c":"api","q":"How do you validate REST API status code, headers and response body?","a":"<p>Three layers of validation: status code, headers, and body — in every API test.</p>\n<pre><code>// Playwright API Testing\ntest('GET /api/users returns valid response', async ({ request }) => {\n  const response = await request.get('https://api.example.com/users', {\n    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }\n  });\n\n  // 1. Status Code\n  expect(response.status()).toBe(200);\n  expect(response.ok()).toBeTruthy();\n\n  const body = await response.json();\n\n  // 2. Headers\n  expect(response.headers()['content-type']).toContain('application/json');\n\n  // 3. Response Body\n  expect(body).toHaveProperty('data');\n  expect(Array.isArray(body.data)).toBe(true);\n  expect(body.data.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);\n  expect(body.data[0]).toMatchObject({\n    id: expect.any(Number),\n    email: expect.stringContaining('@')\n  });\n});\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>// Rest Assured (Java)\ngiven()\n    .header(\"Authorization\", \"Bearer \" + token)\n    .contentType(ContentType.JSON)\n.when()\n    .get(\"/api/users\")\n.then()\n    .statusCode(200)\n    .contentType(ContentType.JSON)\n    .header(\"X-Request-Id\", notNullValue())\n    .body(\"data\", not(empty()))\n    .body(\"data[0].id\", notNullValue())\n    .body(\"data[0].email\", containsString(\"@\"))\n    .time(lessThan(2000L)); // response time SLA\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Don't stop at 200</b>A 200 with an empty body or wrong Content-Type is a defect. Always validate the structure and key values.</div>"},
{"c":"sql","q":"Write an SQL query to find duplicate records.","a":"<p>Use <code>GROUP BY</code> with <code>HAVING COUNT(*) > 1</code> to find duplicates.</p>\n<pre><code>-- 1. Find duplicate email addresses in users table\nSELECT email, COUNT(*) AS occurrence\nFROM   users\nGROUP  BY email\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1\nORDER  BY occurrence DESC;\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>-- 2. View the full duplicate rows\nSELECT *\nFROM   users\nWHERE  email IN (\n    SELECT email\n    FROM   users\n    GROUP  BY email\n    HAVING COUNT(*) > 1\n)\nORDER  BY email;\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>-- 3. Duplicates on multiple columns (composite key)\nSELECT first_name, last_name, dob, COUNT(*) AS cnt\nFROM   customers\nGROUP  BY first_name, last_name, dob\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1;\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>-- 4. Delete duplicates keeping only the latest (using CTE + ROW_NUMBER)\nWITH ranked AS (\n    SELECT id,\n           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY email ORDER BY created_at DESC) AS rn\n    FROM   users\n)\nDELETE FROM users\nWHERE  id IN (SELECT id FROM ranked WHERE rn > 1);\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>In automation</b>Run this query after bulk data operations to assert data integrity: no duplicate orders, no duplicate user registrations.</div>"},
{"c":"sql","q":"How do you validate UI data with Database values?","a":"<p>Extract the value from the UI, query the DB with the same key, and assert they match.</p>\n<pre><code>// Playwright + Node.js with pg (PostgreSQL)\nimport { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';\nimport { Pool } from 'pg';\n\nconst db = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DB_URL });\n\ntest('Order total on UI matches database', async ({ page }) => {\n  const orderId = '12345';\n  await page.goto(`/orders/${orderId}`);\n\n  // Step 1: Read value from UI\n  const uiTotal = await page.getByTestId('order-total').textContent();\n  const uiAmount = parseFloat(uiTotal!.replace('$', '').trim());\n\n  // Step 2: Query the database\n  const result = await db.query(\n    'SELECT total_amount FROM orders WHERE order_id = $1',\n    [orderId]\n  );\n  const dbAmount = parseFloat(result.rows[0].total_amount);\n\n  // Step 3: Assert equality\n  expect(uiAmount).toBeCloseTo(dbAmount, 2); // 2 decimal places\n\n  await db.end();\n});\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>// Java (Selenium + JDBC)\nString uiText = driver.findElement(By.id(\"order-total\")).getText(); // \"$199.99\"\ndouble uiAmount = Double.parseDouble(uiText.replace(\"$\", \"\"));\n\nConnection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(DB_URL, DB_USER, DB_PASS);\nPreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(\n    \"SELECT total_amount FROM orders WHERE order_id = ?\"\n);\nps.setString(1, orderId);\nResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery();\ndouble dbAmount = rs.next() ? rs.getDouble(\"total_amount\") : 0;\n\nassertEquals(uiAmount, dbAmount, 0.01, \"UI and DB amounts should match\");\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Design pattern</b>Wrap DB access in a helper class <code>DBUtil</code> — tests call <code>DBUtil.getOrderTotal(orderId)</code>. Keeps connection logic out of test code.</div>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"Explain your Playwright framework architecture.","a":"<p>A production Playwright framework follows a layered architecture — each layer has one responsibility.</p>\n<pre><code>playwright-framework/\n├── playwright.config.ts       # Base URL, workers, retries, reporters\n├── global-setup.ts            # One-time login, storageState generation\n├── pages/                     # Page Object Model\n│   ├── BasePage.ts            # Shared helpers (navigate, waitForLoad)\n│   ├── LoginPage.ts\n│   ├── DashboardPage.ts\n│   └── CheckoutPage.ts\n├── tests/                     # Test files\n│   ├── smoke/\n│   ├── regression/\n│   └── api/\n├── fixtures/                  # Custom fixtures extending base test\n│   └── customFixtures.ts\n├── utils/\n│   ├── ApiHelper.ts           # REST API utility for test setup/teardown\n│   ├── DBHelper.ts            # DB validation utility\n│   └── TestDataFactory.ts     # Dynamic data generation\n├── test-data/\n│   ├── users.json\n│   └── products.json\n├── auth/\n│   └── storageState.json      # Saved auth session\n└── reports/\n    ├── html-report/\n    └── allure-results/\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>// playwright.config.ts\nexport default defineConfig({\n  baseURL: process.env.BASE_URL || 'https://staging.app.com',\n  workers: process.env.CI ? 4 : 2,\n  fullyParallel: true,\n  retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0,\n  use: {\n    trace: 'on-first-retry',\n    screenshot: 'only-on-failure',\n    video: 'retain-on-failure',\n    storageState: 'auth/storageState.json'\n  },\n  reporter: [['html'], ['allure-playwright'], ['junit', {outputFile:'results.xml'}]]\n});\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Explain the WHY behind each folder — interviewers want to know you understand maintainability and scalability, not just that you created folders.</div>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"Why did you choose Playwright over Selenium?","a":"<table>\n<tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Playwright</th><th>Selenium</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Protocol</td><td>Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP)</td><td>WebDriver HTTP protocol</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Auto-waiting</td><td>Built-in for all actions</td><td>Manual explicit waits required</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Flakiness</td><td>Lower (auto-wait + auto-retry)</td><td>Higher (manual sync required)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Parallelism</td><td>Worker processes, browser contexts</td><td>ThreadLocal + Grid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Network mocking</td><td>Built-in page.route()</td><td>Third-party (BrowserMob, WireMock)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Debugging</td><td>Trace Viewer, UI Mode, Inspector</td><td>Screenshots, logs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Language</td><td>JS/TS, Python, Java, C#</td><td>Java, Python, C#, Ruby, JS</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Test runner</td><td>Built-in (@playwright/test)</td><td>External (TestNG, JUnit)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Key reasons for choosing Playwright:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Auto-waiting eliminates most flakiness without explicit wait boilerplate</li>\n<li>Browser contexts enable cheap parallel isolation without Grid overhead</li>\n<li>storageState saves login session — massive time saving in regression suites</li>\n<li>Built-in API testing eliminates the need for a separate Rest Assured setup for simple validations</li>\n<li>Trace Viewer provides post-failure forensics that screenshots alone can't give</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Honest answer</b>Selenium still wins for teams with existing Java investment, enterprise Grid infrastructure, or needing Appium-style mobile drivers. The choice depends on context.</div>"},
{"c":"lead","q":"How have you implemented Page Object Model?","a":"<p>POM separates locators and page interactions from test logic — a UI change is a one-file fix.</p>\n<pre><code>// pages/BasePage.ts\nimport { Page, Locator } from '@playwright/test';\n\nexport class BasePage {\n  constructor(protected page: Page) {}\n\n  async navigateTo(path: string) {\n    await this.page.goto(path);\n  }\n\n  async waitForNetworkIdle() {\n    await this.page.waitForLoadState('networkidle');\n  }\n\n  async getToastMessage(): Promise&lt;string&gt; {\n    return await this.page.getByRole('alert').textContent() ?? '';\n  }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>// pages/LoginPage.ts\nimport { Page, expect } from '@playwright/test';\nimport { BasePage } from './BasePage';\n\nexport class LoginPage extends BasePage {\n  private readonly emailInput: Locator;\n  private readonly passwordInput: Locator;\n  private readonly submitBtn: Locator;\n\n  constructor(page: Page) {\n    super(page);\n    this.emailInput   = page.getByLabel('Email');\n    this.passwordInput = page.getByLabel('Password');\n    this.submitBtn    = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign In' });\n  }\n\n  async login(email: string, password: string) {\n    await this.navigateTo('/login');\n    await this.emailInput.fill(email);\n    await this.passwordInput.fill(password);\n    await this.submitBtn.click();\n  }\n\n  async getErrorMessage(): Promise&lt;string&gt; {\n    return await this.page.getByRole('alert').textContent() ?? '';\n  }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>// tests/login.spec.ts\ntest('valid login redirects to dashboard', async ({ page }) => {\n  const loginPage = new LoginPage(page);\n  await loginPage.login('admin@app.com', 'Password1!');\n  await expect(page).toHaveURL(/dashboard/);\n});\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Anti-patterns to avoid</b>No assertions inside page objects (pages return data; tests assert). No waits in test files (waits belong in page methods). No driver calls directly in tests.</div>"},
{"c":"lead","q":"Explain your folder structure in the framework.","a":"<pre><code>playwright-framework/\n├── playwright.config.ts        # Central config: base URL, workers, reporters\n├── global-setup.ts             # Runs once before all tests: auth setup\n├── global-teardown.ts\n│\n├── pages/                      # Page Object Model\n│   ├── BasePage.ts             # Shared helper methods\n│   ├── LoginPage.ts\n│   ├── DashboardPage.ts\n│   └── components/             # Reusable UI components\n│       ├── Header.ts\n│       └── Modal.ts\n│\n├── tests/                      # Test specs grouped by feature\n│   ├── auth/\n│   │   └── login.spec.ts\n│   ├── checkout/\n│   │   └── payment.spec.ts\n│   └── api/\n│       └── orders.api.spec.ts  # Pure API tests\n│\n├── fixtures/                   # Custom test fixtures\n│   └── customFixtures.ts       # Extend base test with POM instances\n│\n├── utils/\n│   ├── ApiHelper.ts            # HTTP request helpers for test setup\n│   ├── DBHelper.ts             # Database queries for validation\n│   ├── TestDataFactory.ts      # Dynamic test data generators\n│   └── DateUtils.ts\n│\n├── test-data/\n│   ├── users.json\n│   └── products.json\n│\n├── auth/\n│   └── storageState.json       # Saved login session (gitignored)\n│\n├── .env                        # Environment variables (gitignored)\n├── .env.staging\n└── reports/\n    ├── html-report/\n    └── allure-results/\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Key decisions</b>The <code>fixtures/</code> folder wires the pages into tests automatically. The <code>utils/</code> folder keeps infrastructure code (DB, API) away from test logic. Env vars never in source control.</div>"},
{"c":"lead","q":"How do you identify which test cases should be automated?","a":"<p>Use the ROI formula: <strong>Time Saved = (Manual execution time × Run frequency) − (Build + Maintenance cost)</strong>. Automate when the number is positive.</p>\n<p><strong>Automate:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Regression suite — runs every release, high volume</li>\n<li>Smoke tests — runs on every commit, must be fast and reliable</li>\n<li>Data-driven tests — same flow, 50+ data sets (login with different users)</li>\n<li>Cross-browser / cross-device — tedious manually, trivial with parallelism</li>\n<li>High-risk business flows — payment, checkout, login (always run)</li>\n<li>Performance-sensitive flows — API response time checks</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Keep manual:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Exploratory testing — requires human creativity and intuition</li>\n<li>Usability / accessibility — requires human judgment</li>\n<li>One-time or rarely run tests</li>\n<li>Features in active development — UI changes weekly, ROI is negative</li>\n<li>Visual design validation — pixel-perfect checks (unless using visual testing tools)</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>// Decision matrix\nScore each candidate test:\n  Execution frequency: daily=3, weekly=2, monthly=1\n  Manual effort (mins): >30=3, 10-30=2, <10=1  \n  Stability: stable=3, changing=1\n  Risk level: critical=3, medium=2, low=1\n\nAutomate if score > 8, Manual if < 5, Review if 5-8</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Review automation candidates at sprint planning. If a feature's requirements change every sprint, automating it now creates maintenance debt that outweighs the benefit.</div>"},
{"c":"lead","q":"How do you manage test data in your framework?","a":"<p>Test data is external, environment-scoped, and never hardcoded in tests.</p>\n<pre><code>// Strategy 1: JSON files for fixed test data\n// test-data/users.json\n{\n  \"admin\": { \"email\": \"admin@app.com\", \"password\": \"Admin123!\" },\n  \"readOnly\": { \"email\": \"viewer@app.com\", \"password\": \"View123!\" }\n}\n\n// In test\nimport testData from '../test-data/users.json';\nawait loginPage.login(testData.admin.email, testData.admin.password);\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>// Strategy 2: API setup — create data before test, clean after\ntest('checkout with new order', async ({ request, page }) => {\n  // Setup: create order via API (fast, no UI clicks)\n  const order = await request.post('/api/orders', {\n    data: { productId: 'P001', quantity: 2 }\n  });\n  const { orderId } = await order.json();\n\n  // Test the UI with real data\n  await page.goto(`/orders/${orderId}`);\n  await expect(page.getByTestId('order-status')).toHaveText('Pending');\n\n  // Teardown: delete test data\n  await request.delete(`/api/orders/${orderId}`);\n});\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>// Strategy 3: Dynamic data with faker\nimport { faker } from '@faker-js/faker';\n\nconst newUser = {\n  name:  faker.person.fullName(),\n  email: `qa+${Date.now()}@test.com`, // guaranteed unique\n  phone: faker.phone.number()\n};\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>// Strategy 4: Environment-specific config\n// .env.staging\nBASE_URL=https://staging.app.com\nDB_URL=postgres://staging-db:5432/appdb\nADMIN_EMAIL=admin@staging.app.com\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Golden rule</b>Tests should be self-sufficient — create what they need, assert, clean up. Never depend on data another test created or on manually seeded records.</div>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"How do you execute Playwright tests in parallel?","a":"<p>Playwright parallelises at the worker level — each worker is an independent Node.js process with its own browser.</p>\n<pre><code>// playwright.config.ts\nexport default defineConfig({\n  // Workers: number of parallel browser processes\n  workers: process.env.CI ? 4 : 2,\n\n  // fullyParallel: tests within same file also run in parallel\n  fullyParallel: true,\n});\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>// Shard across CI machines (e.g. 4 GitHub Actions runners)\n// Machine 1: npx playwright test --shard=1/4\n// Machine 2: npx playwright test --shard=2/4\n// Machine 3: npx playwright test --shard=3/4\n// Machine 4: npx playwright test --shard=4/4\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code># GitHub Actions with sharding\njobs:\n  test:\n    strategy:\n      matrix:\n        shard: [1, 2, 3, 4]\n    steps:\n      - run: npx playwright test --shard=${{ matrix.shard }}/4\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>// Browser contexts provide isolation without extra processes\n// Two users in one test, no interference\nconst adminContext = await browser.newContext({ storageState: 'admin.json' });\nconst userContext  = await browser.newContext({ storageState: 'user.json' });\nconst adminPage = await adminContext.newPage();\nconst userPage  = await userContext.newPage();\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Parallel safety</b>Tests must be independent — no shared state, no sequential dependencies. Each test creates its own data via API setup. Parallel tests that share data are a flakiness time bomb.</div>"},
{"c":"devops","q":"How do you integrate Playwright with Jenkins?","a":"<p>Playwright runs in any CI environment — Jenkins needs Node.js, a headless browser, and a way to publish the report.</p>\n<pre><code>// Jenkinsfile (Declarative Pipeline)\npipeline {\n  agent any\n\n  tools { nodejs '18' }\n\n  environment {\n    BASE_URL  = credentials('staging-base-url')\n    AUTH_TOKEN = credentials('api-token')\n  }\n\n  stages {\n    stage('Install') {\n      steps {\n        sh 'npm ci'\n        sh 'npx playwright install --with-deps chromium'\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage('Run Tests') {\n      steps {\n        sh 'npx playwright test --reporter=html,junit'\n      }\n      post {\n        always {\n          // Publish JUnit XML for Jenkins test dashboard\n          junit 'results.xml'\n\n          // Archive HTML report as build artifact\n          publishHTML target: [\n            allowMissing: false,\n            alwaysLinkToLastBuild: true,\n            keepAll: true,\n            reportDir: 'playwright-report',\n            reportFiles: 'index.html',\n            reportName: 'Playwright Report'\n          ]\n\n          // Archive traces and screenshots\n          archiveArtifacts artifacts: 'test-results/**', allowEmptyArchive: true\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Key points</b>Run <code>playwright install --with-deps</code> — Jenkins agents rarely have browsers pre-installed. Use Jenkins credentials() for secrets — never hardcode tokens in Jenkinsfile.</div>"},
{"c":"devops","q":"How do you integrate Playwright with Azure DevOps?","a":"<p>Azure DevOps uses YAML pipelines — the steps are: install, run, and publish results.</p>\n<pre><code># azure-pipelines.yml\ntrigger:\n  branches:\n    include: [main, develop]\n\npool:\n  vmImage: 'ubuntu-latest'\n\nvariables:\n  BASE_URL: $(STAGING_URL)   # Set in ADO Library / Variable Group\n\nsteps:\n  - task: NodeTool@0\n    inputs:\n      versionSpec: '18.x'\n\n  - script: npm ci\n    displayName: 'Install dependencies'\n\n  - script: npx playwright install --with-deps\n    displayName: 'Install Playwright browsers'\n\n  - script: |\n      npx playwright test \\\n        --reporter=html,junit \\\n        --workers=4\n    displayName: 'Run Playwright tests'\n    env:\n      BASE_URL: $(BASE_URL)\n      AUTH_TOKEN: $(AUTH_TOKEN)  # From ADO secret variable\n    continueOnError: true        # Publish results even if tests fail\n\n  # Publish JUnit results to ADO test dashboard\n  - task: PublishTestResults@2\n    condition: always()\n    inputs:\n      testResultsFormat: 'JUnit'\n      testResultsFiles: 'results.xml'\n      testRunTitle: 'Playwright Tests'\n      failTaskOnFailedTests: true\n\n  # Publish HTML report as pipeline artifact\n  - task: PublishPipelineArtifact@1\n    condition: always()\n    inputs:\n      targetPath: playwright-report\n      artifact: 'playwright-html-report'\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Tip</b>Store secrets (BASE_URL, tokens, DB passwords) in Azure DevOps Library → Variable Groups → Link to pipeline. Never in YAML files. Use <code>continueOnError: true</code> before publish tasks so results are always uploaded, even on failure.</div>"},
{"c":"devops","q":"How do you generate Allure or HTML reports?","a":"<p>Playwright ships with a built-in HTML reporter. Allure provides richer history, trends, and integrations.</p>\n<pre><code>// ── Built-in HTML Reporter ──\n// playwright.config.ts\nreporter: [\n  ['html', { open: 'never', outputFolder: 'playwright-report' }],\n  ['junit', { outputFile: 'results.xml' }],\n  ['list']   // console output during test run\n]\n\n// Generate + open\n// npx playwright test                   (generates report)\n// npx playwright show-report            (opens browser)\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>// ── Allure Reporter ──\n// 1. Install\nnpm install -D allure-playwright\n\n// 2. playwright.config.ts\nreporter: [\n  ['allure-playwright', {\n    detail: true,\n    outputFolder: 'allure-results',\n    suiteTitle: false\n  }]\n]\n\n// 3. Add metadata to tests\nimport { test, allure } from 'allure-playwright';\ntest('user can checkout', async ({ page }) => {\n  allure.story('Checkout');\n  allure.severity('critical');\n  allure.step('Navigate to cart', async () => {\n    await page.goto('/cart');\n  });\n});\n\n// 4. Generate HTML from results\n// npx allure generate allure-results --clean -o allure-report\n// npx allure open allure-report\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code># GitHub Actions: archive and serve Allure report\n- name: Generate Allure Report\n  run: npx allure generate allure-results -o allure-report\n\n- name: Upload Allure Report\n  uses: actions/upload-artifact@v3\n  with:\n    name: allure-report\n    path: allure-report\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Allure vs HTML</b>HTML report: zero setup, great for sharing single runs. Allure: add history/trend tracking, integrate with Jira, BETTER for stakeholder reporting. Use Allure for mature frameworks; built-in HTML for quick setups.</div>"},
{"c":"lead","q":"How do you identify and fix flaky tests?","a":"<p>Flaky tests fail intermittently without code changes — treat them as defects with an owner, not random noise.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: Detect &amp; measure</strong></p>\n<pre><code>// playwright.config.ts — retries expose flakiness\nretries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0\n// A test that passes on retry = flaky (Playwright marks it as such)\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Classify the root cause</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Category</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Timing</td><td>Element not ready when action fires</td><td>Use auto-waiting; avoid page.waitForTimeout()</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Test isolation</td><td>Tests share state or data</td><td>Independent setup; API-created data with unique IDs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Environment</td><td>Slow CI machines, network latency</td><td>Increase timeout; use stable test environment</td></tr>\n<tr><td>App race condition</td><td>Backend async behaviour</td><td>Wait for API response, not just UI update</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Selector fragility</td><td>CSS class or DOM structure changes</td><td>Switch to getByRole / getByTestId</td></tr>\n</table>\n<pre><code>// Common fixes\n// ❌ Wrong: fixed sleep\nawait page.waitForTimeout(3000);\n\n// ✅ Right: wait for specific condition\nawait page.waitForResponse('**/api/checkout');\nawait expect(page.getByTestId('success-msg')).toBeVisible();\n\n// ❌ Wrong: brittle CSS class selector\nawait page.locator('.btn-primary-v2-enabled').click();\n\n// ✅ Right: semantic locator\nawait page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Confirm Order' }).click();\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Quarantine if unrepairable quickly</strong></p>\n<pre><code>test.skip('payment flaky on CI — tracked in JIRA-123', async ({ page }) => {\n  // Fix deadline: next sprint\n});\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Lead angle</b>Track a weekly flaky test count as a team metric. A suite with 15%+ flaky tests loses developer trust — people start ignoring red builds. Trust in the suite is the most valuable thing to protect.</div>"},
{"c":"java","q":"Write a Java program to remove duplicate values from an array or list.","a":"<p>In automation frameworks and Java technical interviews, you should know how to deduplicate both collections and primitive arrays.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Approach 1: Using <code>LinkedHashSet</code> (Maintains Insertion Order — Most Common in Frameworks)</strong></p>\n<pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Deduplication {\n    public static &lt;T&gt; List&lt;T&gt; removeDuplicates(List&lt;T&gt; list) {\n        if (list == null || list.isEmpty()) return Collections.emptyList();\n        // LinkedHashSet removes duplicates while preserving original order\n        return new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(new LinkedHashSet&lt;&gt;(list));\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;String&gt; browserLogs = Arrays.asList(\"Chrome\", \"Firefox\", \"Chrome\", \"Safari\", \"Firefox\");\n        List&lt;String&gt; uniqueBrowsers = removeDuplicates(browserLogs);\n        System.out.println(uniqueBrowsers); // [Chrome, Firefox, Safari]\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n\n<p><strong>Approach 2: Java 8+ Streams (<code>.distinct()</code>)</strong></p>\n<pre><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; testData = Arrays.asList(10, 20, 10, 30, 40, 20, 50);\n\nList&lt;Integer&gt; uniqueData = testData.stream()\n    .distinct()\n    .collect(Collectors.toList());\n\nSystem.out.println(uniqueData); // [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]</code></pre>\n\n<p><strong>Approach 3: In-Place Two Pointers on Sorted Primitive Array (O(1) Space DSA Question)</strong></p>\n<pre><code>// Modifies array in-place, returns count of unique elements\npublic static int removeDuplicatesSorted(int[] nums) {\n    if (nums.length == 0) return 0;\n\n    int uniqueIndex = 0; // Pointer for placement of next unique element\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt; nums.length; i++) {\n        if (nums[i] != nums[uniqueIndex]) {\n            uniqueIndex++;\n            nums[uniqueIndex] = nums[i];\n        }\n    }\n    return uniqueIndex + 1; // Length of unique portion\n}\n\nint[] arr = {1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4};\nint length = removeDuplicatesSorted(arr);\n// Unique elements reside in arr[0 .. length-1] -&gt; [1, 2, 3, 4]</code></pre>\n\n<p><strong>Approach 4: Deduplicating POJOs / Objects by a Specific Property (e.g., User ID)</strong></p>\n<pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;\nimport java.util.function.Function;\nimport java.util.function.Predicate;\n\npublic record User(int id, String name) {}\n\npublic static &lt;T&gt; Predicate&lt;T&gt; distinctByKey(Function&lt;? super T, ?&gt; keyExtractor) {\n    Set&lt;Object&gt; seen = ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet();\n    return t -&gt; seen.add(keyExtractor.apply(t));\n}\n\n// In automation test verifying API response users:\nList&lt;User&gt; users = Arrays.asList(\n    new User(101, \"Alice\"),\n    new User(102, \"Bob\"),\n    new User(101, \"Alice (Duplicate ID)\")\n);\n\nList&lt;User&gt; uniqueUsers = users.stream()\n    .filter(distinctByKey(User::id))\n    .collect(Collectors.toList());\n// Result contains only user 101 and 102</code></pre>\n\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Automation Use Case</b>\nUse this to assert UI table columns against database or API payloads: \n<code>Assert.assertEquals(extractedTableList.size(), new HashSet&lt;&gt;(extractedTableList).size(), \"Duplicates found in dropdown/table!\");</code>\n</div>"},
{"c":"sql","q":"Write an SQL query to find the second highest salary.","a":"<p>Classic SQL interview question — multiple approaches, each with trade-offs.</p>\n<pre><code>-- Approach 1: Subquery (most common answer)\nSELECT MAX(salary) AS second_highest_salary\nFROM   employees\nWHERE  salary &lt; (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees);\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>-- Approach 2: DENSE_RANK (handles ties correctly)\nSELECT salary AS second_highest_salary\nFROM (\n    SELECT salary,\n           DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk\n    FROM employees\n) ranked\nWHERE rnk = 2\nLIMIT 1;\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>-- Approach 3: OFFSET (simple but not null-safe)\nSELECT DISTINCT salary\nFROM   employees\nORDER  BY salary DESC\nLIMIT  1 OFFSET 1;\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>-- Approach 4: Nth highest (generalised solution)\n-- Replace N with the desired rank\nSELECT salary AS nth_highest\nFROM (\n    SELECT salary,\n           DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk\n    FROM employees\n) t\nWHERE rnk = 2; -- change 2 to N for any rank\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Edge cases</b>What if there are only 1 distinct salary? The subquery returns NULL — that's correct. DENSE_RANK handles ties: if two employees share the top salary, both get rank 1 and the next salary gets rank 2. ROW_NUMBER would assign different ranks to tied salaries — wrong for this use case.</div>"},
{"c":"playwright","q":"A Playwright test passes locally but fails in Jenkins. How will you debug it?","a":"<p>Environment differences are the #1 cause of local-pass/CI-fail issues. Work through these layers systematically.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: Capture artifacts from the failing CI run</strong></p>\n<pre><code>// playwright.config.ts — ensure these are set\nuse: {\n  trace:      'on-first-retry',\n  screenshot: 'only-on-failure',\n  video:      'retain-on-failure'\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Diagnose common causes</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Cause</th><th>Symptom</th><th>Fix</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Headless vs headed mode</td><td>JS/CSS behaves differently</td><td>Ensure headless: true locally too; test both modes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Slower CI machine</td><td>Timeout errors</td><td>Increase timeout in config or per-test</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Missing env vars</td><td>Auth failures, wrong base URL</td><td>Check Jenkins credentials mapping</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Browser not installed</td><td>Browser launch error</td><td>Add <code>npx playwright install --with-deps</code> step</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Race condition / timing</td><td>Element not found</td><td>Use proper waits, not fixed timeouts</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Different viewport</td><td>Element off-screen</td><td>Set explicit viewport in config</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Test order dependency</td><td>Passes alone, fails in suite</td><td>Make tests independent; reset state in beforeEach</td></tr>\n</table>\n<pre><code>// Increase timeout for CI\ntest.setTimeout(60000); // 60s for CI (default 30s)\n\n// Or globally in config\nexport default defineConfig({\n  timeout: 60000,\n  expect: { timeout: 10000 }\n});\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code># Useful Jenkins debugging: run with verbose output\nnpx playwright test --reporter=list --workers=1 2>&amp;1 | tee test.log\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Golden rule</b>Never investigate a CI failure without the trace file. <code>npx playwright show-trace trace.zip</code> gives you the full DOM state, network calls, and console errors at the exact moment of failure — no guessing needed.</div>"},
{"c":"lead","q":"Your automation suite started failing after a deployment. What steps will you take?","a":"<p>Treat it as an investigation with two hypotheses: the app changed, or the suite changed.</p>\n<p><strong>Immediate triage (first 30 minutes):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Check the failure pattern</strong> — is it one test, one module, or the entire suite? Scattered failures = environment issue. Clustered failures = feature-specific regression.</li>\n<li><strong>Compare with last green run</strong> — what changed in the deployment? (release notes, changelog, diff)</li>\n<li><strong>Re-run failed tests once</strong> — if they pass on retry, it's flakiness or an environment warm-up issue.</li>\n<li><strong>Check environment health</strong> — is the staging environment actually up? Are all services healthy? (status page, smoke test)</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Root cause analysis:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>// Common failure signals from trace/screenshots:\n// 1. Selector not found → UI element changed (locator update needed)\n// 2. Wrong page URL → routing or redirect changed\n// 3. API 401/403 → auth token or endpoint changed\n// 4. Assertion fails on text → copy/label changed\n// 5. Timeout → new loading spinner, slower DB query post-deploy\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Actions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>If app regression</strong> → raise a defect, block the release, notify the team. Don't update the test to pass a broken app.</li>\n<li><strong>If intentional app change</strong> → update the tests, get them passing, merge the fix before or alongside the feature.</li>\n<li><strong>If environment issue</strong> → work with DevOps to stabilise. Run against a known-good environment to confirm.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Process tip</b>Set up a Slack/Teams notification from Jenkins so the team knows within minutes of a suite failure. Stale failure notifications cause alert fatigue — configure them to fire on state changes (pass → fail), not on every failure.</div>"},
{"c":"lead","q":"Tell me about the most challenging automation problem you solved.","a":"<p>Structure your answer using STAR (Situation, Task, Action, Result). Here's a model answer — personalise with your actual experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Example Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Situation:</strong> Our checkout automation suite was failing 40% of the time in CI. The tests all passed locally, but Jenkins runs were consistently red. The team had lost trust in the suite and started ignoring failures.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> Identify and fix the flakiness, restore trust, and do it without blocking the sprint.</p>\n<p><strong>Action:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enabled trace recording on CI (<code>trace: 'on-first-retry'</code>) to capture failure forensics</li>\n<li>Analysed 20 trace files — found three root causes:\n  <ol>\n    <li><strong>Race condition:</strong> Tests asserted UI update before the API call completed. Fixed by replacing <code>waitForTimeout(2000)</code> with <code>waitForResponse('**/checkout')</code></li>\n    <li><strong>Shared test data:</strong> Parallel tests were hitting the same coupon code, which could only be used once. Fixed by generating unique coupon codes via API in beforeEach.</li>\n    <li><strong>Timing on slow CI:</strong> Default 30s timeout was too short on our underpowered Jenkins agent. Increased CI timeout to 60s and raised a request to upgrade the agent.</li>\n  </ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong> Flaky rate dropped from 40% to under 2% within two weeks. The team resumed trusting the pipeline, and we were able to reinstate the CI gate that had been removed during the flakiness crisis.</p>\n<pre><code>// The fix that made the biggest difference\n// Before (race condition)\nawait page.click('#checkout-confirm');\nawait page.waitForTimeout(2000); // hope the API is done\nawait expect(page.locator('#order-number')).toBeVisible();\n\n// After (deterministic)\nawait Promise.all([\n  page.waitForResponse(resp =&gt;\n    resp.url().includes('/api/checkout') &amp;&amp; resp.status() === 200\n  ),\n  page.click('#checkout-confirm')\n]);\nawait expect(page.getByTestId('order-number')).toBeVisible();\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Interview tip</b>The interviewer wants to hear about YOUR problem-solving process, not just the solution. Emphasise: how you diagnosed it, what data you used, how you validated the fix, and what you changed in process to prevent recurrence.</div>"}
];
